consiSt("pa",{"courts":[{"l":"HIGHEST COURT","n":"Supreme Court of Pennsylvania","d":"The court of last resort, with administrative authority over the whole Unified Judicial System. It reviews the intermediate courts mostly by discretionary petition for allowance of appeal (allocatur). It also hears some direct appeals, including appeals as of right from matters that began in the Commonwealth Court. It makes the procedural rules and regulates admission, pro hac vice and lawyer discipline."},{"l":"INTERMEDIATE","n":"Superior Court of Pennsylvania","d":"A statewide intermediate appellate court that hears most civil and criminal appeals from the courts of common pleas."},{"l":"INTERMEDIATE","n":"Commonwealth Court of Pennsylvania","d":"A statewide intermediate appellate court for cases involving the Commonwealth, its agencies and local governments. It hears appeals from the trial courts and from state agencies (by petition for review), and it also has original jurisdiction over some actions brought against the Commonwealth."},{"l":"TRIAL COURTS","n":"Courts of Common Pleas","d":"General-jurisdiction trial courts organized into judicial districts that mostly follow county lines. Civil filings go to the county prothonotary (the civil clerk). Each county adopts its own local rules, including the dollar limit for compulsory arbitration."},{"l":"LIMITED JURISDICTION","n":"Magisterial district courts; Philadelphia Municipal Court; Pittsburgh Municipal Court","d":"The entry-level courts, with more than 500 magisterial districts statewide plus municipal courts in Philadelphia and Pittsburgh. Civil cases before magisterial district judges follow separate rules (Pa.R.Civ.P.M.D.J.), not the Pa.R.Civ.P."}],"slapp":[{"t":"Uniform Public Expression Protection Act (2024): immunity and fees","d":"Act 72 of 2024 (signed July 17, 2024) added 42 Pa.C.S. §§ 8340.11-8340.18. A person is immune from civil liability for a cause of action based on protected public expression if the claimant fails to establish a prima facie case on each element, or fails to state a claim, or if there is no genuine issue of material fact and the defendant is entitled to judgment as a matter of law.\n‘Protected public expression’ means communication in, or on an issue under review in, a legislative, executive, judicial or administrative proceeding. It also covers exercising, on a matter of public concern, the freedoms of speech, press, assembly, petition or association protected by the First Amendment or Pa. Const. art. I, §§ 7 or 20. The statute must be broadly construed.","c":"42 Pa.C.S. §§ 8340.12-8340.13, 8340.15; Act of July 17, 2024, P.L. 836, No. 72","v":true},{"t":"What UPEPA does not cover","d":"The Act excludes claims against, or enforcement claims by, government units and officials acting officially. It also excludes claims against a business arising from communications about its own goods or services (creative and journalistic works are not ‘goods or services’), and bodily injury and death claims unless they sound in defamation, privacy, misappropriation of likeness or emotional distress, or arise solely from public-concern speech. Further exclusions: protection-from-abuse and sexual-violence protection cases; insurance law and insurance-contract claims; trade-secret and corporate-opportunity misappropriation; enforcing nondisparagement or noncompete agreements against a party to them; internal business governance disputes; and manager liability or indemnity.\nIt applies to civil actions commenced, and causes of action asserted, on or after its effective date.","c":"42 Pa.C.S. § 8340.14(b); Act 72 of 2024, § 6","v":true},{"t":"Special motion: 60 days, automatic stay, quick hearing","d":"A party may file a special motion to dismiss, or for judgment on, all or part of a claim based on protected public expression immunity. It is due no later than 60 days after service of the pleading asserting the claim, extendable for good cause. The court hears argument within 60 days after filing and rules, with a written opinion, within 60 days after argument. It decides on the summary-judgment record (Pa.R.Civ.P. 1035.1-1035.2).\nFiling stays all other proceedings, including discovery and the duty to file a responsive pleading, until the order is final, including any appeal. During the stay, parties may still challenge service, jurisdiction or venue, and may voluntarily discontinue. The court may allow limited discovery needed to resolve immunity and may hear urgent public-safety injunction requests.\nThe right to raise immunity in other pleadings and motions is preserved.\nEffective-date caution: under Act 72 § 7(1), § 8340.16 (the special motion) takes effect only on the date given in a Pennsylvania Bulletin notice under § 3(4), triggered by Supreme Court action. The collected Rules of Civil Procedure (current through Sept. 5, 2026) contain no implementing rule, and the Legislature’s site still shows the conditional applicability note. Confirm in the Pennsylvania Bulletin whether the notice has been published. The immunity, fee and appeal sections took effect immediately.","c":"42 Pa.C.S. § 8340.16; Act 72 of 2024, §§ 3, 7","v":true},{"t":"Fees and immediate appeal","d":"If the defendant is immune, the court must award attorney fees, court costs and litigation expenses, jointly and severally against each adverse party that asserted the claim. It must do the same if the claimant voluntarily discontinues (with or without prejudice) after immunity is asserted. If the immunity assertion is frivolous or solely for delay, the court must award the opposing party its fees incurred in opposing it.\nAn order granting, denying or otherwise determining immunity is immediately appealable under 42 Pa.C.S. § 702.\nA separate cause of action lets a person recover fees and costs of the underlying case if that person would have prevailed on immunity but the court never ruled on it. Punitive damages are available on proof that the underlying suit was brought or continued solely to harass, intimidate, punish or maliciously inhibit protected expression.","c":"42 Pa.C.S. §§ 8340.17, 8340.18, 8320.1","v":true},{"t":"Older environmental anti-SLAPP law still applies","d":"A person who files an action to enforce an environmental law, or communicates with a government agency about enforcing or implementing one, is immune from civil liability in a resulting damages action when the action or communication aims to procure favorable government action. The immunity is lost if the allegation or communication is not relevant or material to environmental enforcement or implementation and also is knowingly false or reckless, is made solely to interfere with business relationships, or is later found to be wrongful use or abuse of process.\nThe defendant may move for a hearing on immunity. If the motion is denied, there is an interlocutory appeal of right to the Commonwealth Court, and discovery is stayed during the appeal. A government agency may intervene or participate as amicus. A successful defendant is awarded reasonable fees and costs, with a full or proportionate award on partial success. UPEPA does not affect this statute.","c":"27 Pa.C.S. §§ 7707, 8301-8305; Act 72 of 2024, § 4","v":true}],"oos":[{"t":"Pro hac vice: pay the IOLTA Board first, then a sponsor’s motion","d":"An out-of-state or foreign lawyer may be specially admitted for one case. First, give the Pennsylvania IOLTA Board the required information and pay the $375 fee for each case; it is never refunded. The fee is waived if the client has in forma pauperis status, and for special courts (the Traffic Division of Philadelphia Municipal Court and the Pittsburgh Municipal Court). Pay by check drawn on a U.S. bank, money order or cashier’s check, no later than filing the motion. The IOLTA Board is only the records custodian; the court decides admission.\nThen a Pennsylvania lawyer (the sponsor) files a written motion under Pa.R.Civ.P. 1012.1 with the clerk at least 3 days before the applicant’s first appearance. No oath is required. The admission lasts through final determination, including appeals, unless withdrawal is permitted.","c":"Pa.B.A.R. 301(a)-(b); 204 Pa. Code §§ 81.501-81.506; Pa.R.Civ.P. 1012.1","v":true},{"t":"What the pro hac vice motion must contain","d":"The motion attaches verified statements from both lawyers. The candidate states every jurisdiction of admission and license number, any discipline or pending disciplinary proceedings, the number of pending Pennsylvania pro hac vice applications and any denials, and agrees to be bound by Pennsylvania law and the Rules of Professional Conduct. The candidate also submits to Pennsylvania courts and the Disciplinary Board and appoints the sponsor as agent for service.\nThe sponsor states that the candidate is reputable and competent, gives the number of cases in which the sponsor is acting as sponsor, and states that settlement funds will be handled under Rule 1.15. The court must grant the motion unless it finds good cause to deny, and may revoke admission after an opportunity to respond.","c":"Pa.R.Civ.P. 1012.1","v":true},{"t":"The sponsor stays attorney of record and attends","d":"A lawyer admitted pro hac vice is not authorized to act as attorney of record. The Pennsylvania sponsor enters an appearance and stays attorney of record, signs and receives papers, and attends all proceedings unless excused. The sponsor need not attend depositions unless ordered.","c":"Pa.B.A.R. 301(a); 204 Pa. Code § 81.502(b); Pa.R.Civ.P. 1012.1","v":true},{"t":"Temporary practice without admission","d":"A lawyer admitted elsewhere and not disbarred or suspended may provide temporary services in Pennsylvania: in association with an actively participating Pennsylvania lawyer; in connection with a pending or potential proceeding where the lawyer is or expects to be authorized to appear; in arbitration or mediation reasonably related to the lawyer’s home practice (unless the forum requires pro hac vice); or in other work reasonably related to the home practice. An office or other systematic and continuous presence requires admission, the in-house license, or other authority.","c":"Pa.R.P.C. 5.5(b)-(c)","v":true},{"t":"In-house counsel need a limited license","d":"A lawyer not admitted in Pennsylvania who works in Pennsylvania for a non-governmental business employer on more than a temporary basis, or with an office or continuous presence there, must obtain a Limited In-House Corporate Counsel License. The license is limited to advising and representing that employer and does not cover court appearances that require pro hac vice. It does not authorize lawyers employed by the Commonwealth or its political subdivisions.","c":"Pa.B.A.R. 302; Pa.R.P.C. 5.5(d)","v":true},{"t":"Admission by reciprocity or UBE score transfer","d":"Pennsylvania admits on motion (reciprocity) lawyers on active status in a reciprocal state who meet the education and good-standing requirements. They must have practiced law for a majority of their time in 5 of the last 7 years after passing a reciprocal state’s bar exam or gaining admission there, or meet a UBE-based alternative. A UBE score from another jurisdiction transfers if it meets the Pennsylvania passing score in effect for that exam and no more than 30 months have passed since the exam. Disbarred or suspended lawyers are ineligible.","c":"Pa.B.A.R. 204(b), 206(a)","v":true},{"t":"Limited-scope representation","d":"A lawyer may limit the scope of a representation if the limit is reasonable and the client gives informed consent. The collected civil rules contain no special limited-entry-of-appearance procedure: an attorney who has appeared needs leave of court to withdraw unless substitute counsel enters at the same time and there is no delay. Check county local rules and practice before planning an unbundled court appearance.","c":"Pa.R.P.C. 1.2(c); Pa.R.Civ.P. 1012(b)","v":true},{"t":"E-filing is county by county in trial courts; PACFile on appeal","d":"Each court of common pleas decides by local rule whether to permit or require e-filing. The e-filed document is the original, and the filer must keep the signed paper original for 2 years after the case ends and produce it within 14 days of a request. Electronic service of papers other than original process needs the parties’ agreement or an e-mail address on an appearance or prior filing; an e-mail address on letterhead is not enough.\nAppellate e-filing is through PACFile under Supreme Court administrative orders.","c":"Pa.R.Civ.P. 205.4, 239.9; Pa.R.A.P. 125","v":true}],"ethics":[{"t":"Governing code: Pennsylvania Rules of Professional Conduct","d":"The Pennsylvania Rules of Professional Conduct (204 Pa. Code § 81.4) follow the ABA Model Rules’ numbering and structure, with Pennsylvania-specific changes. They are enforced through the Disciplinary Board of the Supreme Court under the Pennsylvania Rules of Disciplinary Enforcement. A Code of Civility also appears at 204 Pa. Code ch. 99 (not collected here).","c":"204 Pa. Code §§ 81.1-81.4","v":true},{"t":"Fees: writing required for new clients, but no client signature","d":"When the lawyer has not regularly represented the client, the basis or rate of the fee must be communicated in writing before or within a reasonable time after the representation starts. The ABA rule only prefers writing.\nContingent fee agreements must be in writing and must state the method, the percentages for settlement, trial or appeal, and how expenses are handled. Unlike ABA Model Rule 1.5(c), the Pennsylvania text does not require the client to sign. A written closing statement is required at the end.","c":"Pa.R.P.C. 1.5(b)-(c)","v":true},{"t":"Confidentiality: broader permitted disclosures","d":"Disclosure is mandatory where needed to comply with the candor duties of Rule 3.3. Disclosure is permitted, among other grounds, to prevent a client’s criminal act likely to cause substantial financial injury, and to prevent, mitigate or rectify a client’s crime or fraud in which the lawyer’s services were used. Unlike the ABA version, the Pennsylvania text reaches criminal acts without requiring that the lawyer’s services be used for the prevention ground.","c":"Pa.R.P.C. 1.6(b)-(c)","v":true},{"t":"Lateral screening without ABA-style certifications","d":"A firm may avoid imputation of a newly associated lawyer’s former-client conflict by screening that lawyer, giving no part of the fee, and giving prompt written notice to the affected client. The Pennsylvania text does not include the ABA rule’s additional certification and response requirements.","c":"Pa.R.P.C. 1.10(b)","v":true},{"t":"Pennsylvania-only rules: lobbying and investment products","d":"Rule 1.19 requires lawyer-lobbyists to comply with lobbying disclosure laws, treats those disclosures as authorized, and bars former public officers or employees whose service ended on or after June 1, 2023 from lobbying their former body for one year.\nRule 5.8 bars lawyers from selling or placing investment products unless separately licensed, and from recommending them to clients or fiduciary beneficiaries when the lawyer or a related person receives third-party compensation or owns the sponsor.","c":"Pa.R.P.C. 1.19, 5.8","v":true},{"t":"Other text differences","d":"Rule 1.2(e) lets a lawyer counsel or assist a client about conduct expressly permitted by Pennsylvania law, with advice on consequences under other law. Rule 8.4(c) expressly permits supervising lawful investigative activities. Rule 8.4(g) prohibits knowingly engaging, in the practice of law, in harassment or discrimination on listed grounds including gender identity or expression and socioeconomic status.","c":"Pa.R.P.C. 1.2(e), 8.4(c), (g)","v":true}],"style":"Commonwealth of Pennsylvania","home":"https://www.pacourts.us/","unique":{"mines":[{"t":"Confidential Information Form and certification on every filing","d":"Every filing in the trial and appellate courts must certify compliance with the Case Records Public Access Policy. Social Security numbers, financial account numbers (other than the last four digits where needed), driver license and SID numbers, minors’ names and birth dates, and abuse victims’ contact information in family cases go only on a contemporaneously filed Confidential Information Form. The court is not required to redact; compliance is solely the filer’s responsibility.","c":"Pa.R.Civ.P. 205.6; Pa.R.A.P. 127; 204 Pa. Code § 213.81, Policy § 7.0"},{"t":"Preliminary objections, not motions to dismiss; some defenses only as new matter","d":"Rule 12-type challenges are raised by preliminary objections, and all of them must be raised at once. The statute of limitations and statute of frauds cannot be raised by preliminary objection and must be pleaded as new matter. Improper venue is waived if not raised by preliminary objection. A complaint can be amended as of course within 20 days after service of the objections.","c":"Pa.R.Civ.P. 1028, 1030, 1006"},{"t":"A general denial is an admission","d":"Every averment that requires a response is admitted unless specifically denied. A general denial or demand for proof counts as an admission. Lack of knowledge works as a denial only after a stated reasonable investigation. New matter endorsed with a notice to plead must be answered within 20 days or it is admitted.","c":"Pa.R.Civ.P. 1026, 1029"},{"t":"Serve within 30 days in state (90 out of state), or reissue","d":"Original process must be served within 30 days in Pennsylvania and 90 days outside it. If not served in time, it must be reissued or reinstated by praecipe, and the plaintiff must make a good-faith effort to serve, or the filing may not stop the statute of limitations (see the Rule 405 citation to Lamp v. Heyman).","c":"Pa.R.Civ.P. 401, 404"},{"t":"Ten-day notice before default or non pros, every time","d":"The prothonotary cannot enter a default judgment or judgment of non pros by praecipe unless the praecipe certifies that written notice of intent was mailed or delivered at least 10 days earlier, with a copy attached. The notice cannot be waived. For default, it must go to the defaulting party and its counsel.","c":"Pa.R.Civ.P. 237.1, 237.5"},{"t":"Certificate of merit within 60 days in professional liability cases","d":"Any claim that a licensed professional deviated from professional standards needs a certificate of merit for each professional defendant, filed with the complaint or within 60 days. After a defendant’s notice of intent (no earlier than day 31), a missing certificate leads to judgment of non pros 30 days later.","c":"Pa.R.Civ.P. 1042.3, 1042.6, 1042.7"},{"t":"Pro hac vice motion 3 days before appearing, after paying IOLTA","d":"The $375 IOLTA fee per case and the information filing must come first. The sponsor’s Rule 1012.1 motion, with both verified statements and the IOLTA fee certification or averment, must be filed at least 3 days before the out-of-state lawyer appears. The sponsor remains attorney of record.","c":"Pa.B.A.R. 301(b); 204 Pa. Code §§ 81.503(d), 81.505; Pa.R.Civ.P. 1012.1"},{"t":"Post-trial motion within 10 days, then enter judgment","d":"After any civil trial, grounds not raised at trial and specifically stated in a Rule 227.1 motion filed within 10 days are waived on appeal. The appeal runs from the judgment entered on praecipe, not from the order denying post-trial relief. If the motions are undecided after 120 days, a party may have judgment entered.","c":"Pa.R.Civ.P. 227.1, 227.4"},{"t":"Rule 1925(b) Statement: omit an issue and it is gone","d":"If the trial judge orders a concise Statement of errors, file it of record and serve it on the judge within the time ordered (at least 21 days). Every issue not included is waived. The appeal clock is 30 days, is not tolled by a reconsideration motion unless reconsideration is expressly granted within that time, and cannot be extended.","c":"Pa.R.A.P. 1925(b), 1701(b)(3), 903(a), 105(b)"},{"t":"Document subpoenas to non-parties need 20 days’ notice first","d":"Before serving a subpoena for documents on a non-party, give every party 20 days’ written notice with the proposed subpoena. An objection blocks service until the court rules. Federal-style same-day service of notice and subpoena will not work.","c":"Pa.R.Civ.P. 4009.21-4009.24"},{"t":"Check county local rules before filing anything","d":"Motion procedure, briefing, argument lists, preliminary objection and summary judgment practice, e-filing availability, cover sheets and the compulsory arbitration limit all vary by county. Each county also designates the office listed in the notice to defend. Read the county’s published local rules (Pa.R.J.A. 103(d)) before the first filing.","c":"Pa.R.Civ.P. 239-239.9, 1018.1(c), 1301; Pa.R.J.A. 103(d)"},{"t":"Anti-SLAPP special motion: confirm it is in force, and file within 60 days","d":"UPEPA immunity and mandatory fee awards apply to actions filed after July 17, 2024. The special-motion procedure in § 8340.16 (60-day deadline, automatic stay) depends on a Pennsylvania Bulletin notice that this research could not confirm. Plead immunity in preliminary objections, new matter or summary judgment as well, and file any special motion within 60 days of service.","c":"42 Pa.C.S. §§ 8340.15-8340.18; Act 72 of 2024, §§ 3, 6, 7"}],"judge":[{"t":"No peremptory challenge of a judge","d":"The collected Pennsylvania civil and appellate rules provide no peremptory challenge or automatic substitution of a judge (Rule 221’s four peremptory challenges are for jurors). Disqualification follows the Code of Judicial Conduct.","c":"Pa.R.Civ.P. 221; Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.11 (207 Pa. Code ch. 33)","v":true},{"t":"Disqualification standard","d":"A judge must step aside when the judge’s impartiality might reasonably be questioned. Listed examples: personal bias or knowledge of disputed facts; close family relationships to a party, lawyer or material witness; economic interests; public statements committing to a result; prior involvement as a lawyer, government participant or witness; and campaign contributions by a party, lawyer or firm large enough to raise a reasonable concern. There is a rebuttable presumption that contributions at or below the gift-reporting threshold do not require recusal. Except for bias, the parties may waive disqualification after disclosure, outside the judge’s presence.\nThe collected rules set no recusal-motion procedure or deadline. Raise it promptly by motion to the assigned judge, and check case law and local rules.","c":"Code of Judicial Conduct Rules 2.7, 2.11(A), (C); Pa.R.Civ.P. 1006 cmt.","v":true}],"speech":[{"t":"Pa. Const. art. I, §§ 7 and 20","d":"Pennsylvania’s Constitution separately protects free communication, speech and press (art. I, § 7) and assembly and petition (art. I, § 20). The anti-SLAPP statute expressly protects expression guaranteed by these sections as well as by the First Amendment. Whether the state provisions are broader than the First Amendment depends on case law that was not collected or checked here.","c":"Pa. Const. art. I, §§ 7, 20 (not collected); 42 Pa.C.S. § 8340.13","v":false}],"other":[{"t":"County local rules govern a great deal","d":"Each judicial district adopts local rules on motions practice, briefs, preliminary objections, summary judgment procedure, e-filing, cover sheets and the compulsory arbitration limit. The statewide rules require local rules on several of these, numbered to match the statewide rule (Local Rule 208.3(b), 1028(c), 1035.2(a) and so on). A prothonotary may not refuse a filing that complies with the statewide rules only because it fails a local format or cover-sheet rule. Local rules take effect only after publication under Pa.R.J.A. 103(d).","c":"Pa.R.Civ.P. 205.2, 239-239.9; Pa.R.J.A. 103(d)","v":true},{"t":"Fact pleading and verified pleadings","d":"Pennsylvania requires fact pleading: the material facts on which a claim or defense is based must be stated, and claims on a writing must attach it. Every pleading with factual averments must be verified by a party, not counsel, except in narrow cases.","c":"Pa.R.Civ.P. 1019, 1024","v":true},{"t":"Compulsory arbitration below the local limit","d":"Each county sets by local rule which cases go to compulsory arbitration and the dollar limit, within the 42 Pa.C.S. § 7361(b) maximum. An appeal for trial de novo is due within 30 days after the prothonotary notes on the docket that notice of the award was sent. The appellant must pay the arbitrators’ compensation, capped at 50% of the amount in controversy and not recoverable. Fee-and-cost waiver on petition is available from November 1, 2026.","c":"Pa.R.Civ.P. 1301, 1308","v":true},{"t":"Discovery differs from federal practice","d":"There are no automatic initial disclosures. Expert discovery is by interrogatory, with no automatic reports or depositions. A party must give every other party 20 days’ notice before serving a document subpoena on a non-party, and an objection blocks service until the court rules.","c":"Pa.R.Civ.P. 4003.5, 4009.21","v":true}]},"appeals":{"asof":"November 10, 2026","src":"Pennsylvania Rules of Appellate Procedure, 210 Pa. Code (Pa.R.A.P.), Chapters 1-37, from the Pennsylvania Code website as current through 56 Pa.B. 6006 (September 5, 2026); Pennsylvania Rules of Civil Procedure 227.1, 227.4 and 236 (231 Pa. Code); Case Records Public Access Policy (204 Pa. Code § 213.81). The Pennsylvania Code website text is not the official text (see 201 Pa. Code Rule 103); English summaries are Consi’s.","intro":{"t":"Appeals in Pennsylvania: three appellate courts, 30-day notices filed in the trial court, and waiver everywhere","d":"Pennsylvania has two statewide intermediate appellate courts. The Superior Court hears most civil and criminal appeals from the courts of common pleas. The Commonwealth Court hears appeals involving government parties and agencies and some original actions against the Commonwealth. The Supreme Court mostly reviews their decisions by discretionary petition for allowance of appeal (allocatur). Which intermediate court has jurisdiction is set by statute (42 Pa.C.S. §§ 742, 762, not collected here); an appeal filed in the wrong court is transferred, not quashed (Pa.R.A.P. 751).\nA civil appeal is started by a notice of appeal filed with the clerk of the trial court within 30 days after entry of the order. Missing that deadline makes the appeal invalid, and no court may extend it.\nThe biggest traps are about preserving issues: a single post-trial motion within 10 days after a civil trial (Pa.R.Civ.P. 227.1), entry of judgment before appealing (Pa.R.Civ.P. 227.4), and the trial judge’s order to file a Rule 1925(b) Statement of errors, where any issue left out is waived.","c":"Pa.R.A.P. 751, 902, 903, 105(b), 1925(b); Pa.R.Civ.P. 227.1, 227.4"},"groups":[["What can be appealed",[{"t":"Final orders","d":"An appeal lies as of right from a final order. A final order disposes of all claims and all parties, or is certified final under Rule 341(c). There is no federal-style collateral finality for an order resolving only some parties: an order adjudicating fewer than all claims or parties is not final without a Rule 341(c) determination.","c":"Pa.R.A.P. 341(a)-(b)"},{"t":"Partial final orders: Rule 341(c) determination","d":"With multiple claims or parties, the trial court may enter a final order as to fewer than all of them only on an express determination that an immediate appeal would facilitate resolution of the entire case. An application for that determination must be filed within 30 days of entry of the order, the action is stayed while it is pending, and it is deemed denied if the court does not act within 30 days after filing. A denial is reviewable only by a petition for permission to appeal under Rule 1311.","c":"Pa.R.A.P. 341(c)(1)-(3)"},{"t":"Interlocutory appeals as of right","d":"Rule 311 lists orders appealable immediately without a Rule 341(c) determination, including: refusing to open, vacate or strike off a judgment; orders on attachments, receiverships and similar matters affecting possession of property; injunction orders (with exceptions for some orders after trial but before the final order); peremptory judgment in mandamus; an order granting a new trial in a civil case; an order directing partition; orders made appealable by statute or rule; orders changing venue, transferring to a coordinate court or declining jurisdiction on forum non conveniens grounds; and orders overruling preliminary objections to a declaration of taking in eminent domain.","c":"Pa.R.A.P. 311(a), (c), (e)"},{"t":"Orders sustaining venue or personal jurisdiction","d":"An order sustaining venue or personal or in rem jurisdiction is appealable as of right only if (1) the party benefiting from the order files an election within 10 days after its entry that the order be deemed final, or (2) the court states in the order that a substantial issue of venue or jurisdiction is presented. After an election, the notice of appeal is due within 30 days after the election is filed.","c":"Pa.R.A.P. 311(b), 903(c)(2)"},{"t":"Collateral orders","d":"An order separable from and collateral to the main cause of action is appealable as of right if the right involved is too important to be denied review and the claim would be irreparably lost if review waited for final judgment. Appeal it by notice of appeal; the comment says the rule codifies existing case law.","c":"Pa.R.A.P. 313"},{"t":"Interlocutory appeals by permission","d":"For other interlocutory orders, ask the trial court to amend the order to include the statement in 42 Pa.C.S. § 702(b) (a controlling question of law with substantial ground for difference of opinion, where an immediate appeal may materially advance termination). The application is due in the trial court within 30 days after entry of the order and is deemed denied if not acted on within 30 days after filing. Then file a petition for permission to appeal with the appellate prothonotary within 30 days after entry of the amended order, or of the denial or deemed denial of certification.","c":"Pa.R.A.P. 312, 1311(a)-(b)"},{"t":"Anti-SLAPP immunity orders","d":"By statute, an order granting, denying or otherwise determining protected public expression immunity under Pennsylvania’s Uniform Public Expression Protection Act is immediately appealable under 42 Pa.C.S. § 702. The collected appellate rules contain no rule implementing this. Treat it as an appeal to file within the ordinary 30 days and confirm the procedure with the appellate prothonotary.","c":"42 Pa.C.S. § 8340.17"}]],["Preserving issues in the trial court (civil)",[{"t":"One post-trial motion within 10 days, or the issues are waived","d":"After a civil trial (jury or non-jury), all requests for a new trial, judgment notwithstanding the verdict, removal of a nonsuit, or other changes go in a single Motion for Post-Trial Relief. It is due within 10 days after the verdict, discharge of a deadlocked jury, or nonsuit in a jury trial, or after notice of nonsuit or filing of the decision in a non-jury trial. Another party may file its own motion within 10 days after the first is filed. Grounds are waived unless they were raised before or during trial and are specifically stated in the motion, along with how they were raised.","c":"Pa.R.Civ.P. 227.1"},{"t":"Appeal from the judgment, not from the order denying post-trial motions","d":"The prothonotary enters judgment on praecipe when no timely post-trial motion is filed, or when timely motions are filed and the court does not dispose of all of them within 120 days after the first motion is filed. A judgment entered after the 120 days is final as to all parties and issues and is not subject to reconsideration. Enter judgment by praecipe so there is an appealable final order.","c":"Pa.R.Civ.P. 227.4(1)-(2)"},{"t":"Date of entry means the Rule 236 notice date","d":"For civil orders, the date of entry that starts the appeal clock is the day the clerk notes on the docket that written notice of entry was given under Pa.R.Civ.P. 236(b). An order must be entered on the docket, and on a separate document, before it is appealable. A notice of appeal filed after a decision is announced but before an appealable order is entered is treated as filed on the day of entry.","c":"Pa.R.A.P. 108(b), 301(a)-(c), 905(a)(5)"},{"t":"A reconsideration motion does not stop the appeal clock","d":"Filing a motion for reconsideration does not toll the 30 days. The trial court may grant reconsideration after an appeal is filed only if the motion is timely and an order expressly granting reconsideration is filed within the time to appeal. Only then is the notice of appeal inoperative and the 30 days start over after the decision on reconsideration. If the court does not expressly grant reconsideration within 30 days, file the notice of appeal anyway.","c":"Pa.R.A.P. 1701(b)(3)"}]],["Notice of appeal",[{"t":"Where and when to file","d":"File the notice of appeal with the clerk of the trial court (not the appellate court) within 30 days after entry of the order. Missing the deadline makes the appeal invalid. Other defects do not invalidate the appeal but may lead to remand or other action. A notice mistakenly filed in an appellate court or another wrong office is transferred and treated as filed on the original date once an additional filing fee is paid.","c":"Pa.R.A.P. 902(a)-(b), 903(a), 905(a)(4)"},{"t":"One notice per docket","d":"A notice of appeal must be filed in each docket in which the order was entered. A single notice is allowed for completely consolidated civil cases if the consolidation order is attached.","c":"Pa.R.A.P. 902(a), 904(h)"},{"t":"Contents and attachments","d":"Use the form in Rule 904. The caption lists all parties of record in the trial court on the date of appeal, and the notice must state that the order was entered on the docket and attach a copy of the docket entry. Attach the transcript request or counsel’s statement that there is no verbatim record or that the transcript is already of record. File two copies with proof of service and pay the fees, including the appellate docketing fee.","c":"Pa.R.A.P. 904(a)-(d), 905(a)(1), (c), 906"},{"t":"Cross-appeals","d":"Once a timely notice of appeal is filed, any other party may file a notice of appeal within 14 days after the first notice was served, or within the original 30 days, whichever is later. Identify it as a cross-appeal.","c":"Pa.R.A.P. 903(b) & cmt."},{"t":"Shorter periods","d":"Appeals from orders in Election Code matters, Local Government Unit Debt Act matters, and orders changing criminal venue or venire are due within 10 days. The Supreme Court has also temporarily shortened Election Code appeal periods by order for particular elections (for example, to 3 days for the November 5, 2024 general election).","c":"Pa.R.A.P. 903(c)(1); Order No. 622 Judicial Administration Docket (Aug. 27, 2024), 54 Pa.B. 5611"},{"t":"Direct appeals to the Supreme Court: jurisdictional statement","d":"When the appeal goes directly to the Supreme Court, the appellant also files a jurisdictional statement (original and 8 copies) with the trial court clerk. An adverse party may answer within 14 days after service.","c":"Pa.R.A.P. 909(a)-(b)"}]],["Rule 1925: the concise statement",[{"t":"The trial judge writes an opinion after the appeal","d":"After a notice of appeal, the trial judge files an opinion giving the reasons for the order, unless the reasons already appear of record.","c":"Pa.R.A.P. 1925(a)(1)"},{"t":"Statement of errors complained of on appeal","d":"The judge may order the appellant to file of record and serve on the judge a concise Statement of errors. The order must allow at least 21 days from its entry on the docket. Extensions are available for good cause, including a transcript delay not caused by the appellant. A motion to extend because an ordered transcript has not arrived is deemed granted if filed at least 5 days before the due date and not ruled on by then.","c":"Pa.R.A.P. 1925(b), (b)(2)"},{"t":"Issues not in the Statement are waived","d":"The Statement lists only errors the appellant intends to raise, each with enough detail to identify the issue. Each error includes every subsidiary issue raised in the trial court. Issues not included, or not raised in the required way, are waived. If the basis for a civil ruling cannot be discerned, explain that at the start of the Statement so its generality is not held against you. Both filing of record and service on the judge, at the place the order specifies, are required.","c":"Pa.R.A.P. 1925(b)(1), (b)(4)(i)-(vii)"},{"t":"Limited rescue by remand","d":"In a civil case, an appellate court may remand for a Statement nunc pro tunc, or for amendment, only on application and good cause shown. Do not count on it.","c":"Pa.R.A.P. 1925(c)(2)"}]],["Record and briefing",[{"t":"Transcript and record","d":"The appellant must request and pay for the transcript under Pa.R.J.A. 4001 et seq. The record is due in the appellate court within 60 days after the notice of appeal, unless that time is extended.","c":"Pa.R.A.P. 1911(a), 1931(a)(1)"},{"t":"Briefing schedule","d":"Appellant’s brief: 40 days after the record is filed, unless the prothonotary sets another date. Appellee’s brief: 30 days after service of the appellant’s brief and reproduced record. Reply brief: 14 days after service of the preceding brief, and received at least 3 days before argument. In cross-appeals, the designated appellant’s second brief is due 30 days after service of the designated appellee’s brief. Briefs sent by first class, express or priority U.S. mail are filed on the mailing date.","c":"Pa.R.A.P. 2185(a)(1)"},{"t":"Reproduced record","d":"Unless proceeding on a deferred basis, the appellant serves and files a designation of the parts of the record to reproduce, with a short statement of issues, at least 30 days before the brief is due. The appellee may counter-designate within 10 days.","c":"Pa.R.A.P. 2154(a)"},{"t":"Word limits","d":"Principal brief: 14,000 words. Reply brief: 7,000 words. In a cross-appeal, the designated appellee’s brief and the designated appellant’s second brief: 16,500 words each. A certificate of compliance is required if a principal brief exceeds 30 pages or a reply exceeds 15 pages (35 pages for the cross-appeal briefs). Covers, tables, proof of service and addenda do not count. The court may reduce word counts by rule or order.","c":"Pa.R.A.P. 2135(a)-(d) & cmt."},{"t":"Statement of questions involved","d":"No question will be considered unless it is stated in the statement of questions involved or fairly suggested by it. After each question, state whether the lower court agreed, disagreed, or did not answer it.","c":"Pa.R.A.P. 2116(a)"},{"t":"Late briefs","d":"If the appellant misses the deadline for the brief, reproduced record or designation, the appellee may move to dismiss. A late appellee may not be heard at argument without permission.","c":"Pa.R.A.P. 2188"},{"t":"Extensions","d":"An appellate court may extend most deadlines for good cause. It may not extend the time to file a notice of appeal, petition for allowance of appeal, petition for permission to appeal, petition for review, or petition for specialized review.","c":"Pa.R.A.P. 105(b)"}]],["Stays and supersedeas",[{"t":"Effect of appeal","d":"After an appeal, the trial court generally may not proceed further. It may still preserve the status quo, correct formal errors, handle the record, grant supersedeas, enforce orders not superseded, and grant reconsideration under Rule 1701(b)(3).","c":"Pa.R.A.P. 1701(a)-(b)"},{"t":"Automatic supersedeas for money judgments: 120%","d":"An appeal from an order solely for the payment of money operates as a supersedeas once appropriate security of 120% of the unpaid amount is filed with the clerk of the lower court. For installment awards, the amount counted is what is payable within 18 months after entry. Domestic relations money orders need an order and security set by the trial court.","c":"Pa.R.A.P. 1731(a)-(b)"}]],["After decision: reargument and Supreme Court review",[{"t":"Reargument","d":"An application for reargument is due within 14 days after entry of the judgment or order (7 days in children’s fast track appeals) and is limited to 3,000 words. Reargument is discretionary, allowed only for compelling reasons, and not permitted from intermediate appellate final orders under the Election Code or the Local Government Unit Debt Act.","c":"Pa.R.A.P. 2542(a), 2543, 2544(c)"},{"t":"Petition for allowance of appeal (allocatur)","d":"Supreme Court review of the Superior or Commonwealth Court is discretionary. Petition within 30 days after entry of the order, with a limit of 9,000 words (20 pages is deemed compliant). A timely reargument application by any party restarts the 30 days from the order disposing of it. The intermediate court has 60 days to act on reargument (45 days in children’s fast track appeals), after which it is deemed denied. A petition filed while reargument is pending has no effect, so file a new one. Cross-petitions are due within 14 days of service of the first petition. Answers are due within 14 days of service and are limited to 9,000 words.","c":"Pa.R.A.P. 1113(a)-(b), 1115(f), 1116(a), (c)"},{"t":"Grounds for allowance","d":"Allowance is not a matter of right. Listed grounds include: a conflict between intermediate appellate decisions or with the Pennsylvania or U.S. Supreme Court; a question of first impression; substantial public importance; the constitutionality of a Pennsylvania statute; a departure from accepted judicial practice that calls for supervisory review; and an erroneous order quashing or dismissing an appeal.","c":"Pa.R.A.P. 1114(a)-(b)"},{"t":"Appeals as of right from the Commonwealth Court","d":"Rule 1101 provides appeals as of right to the Supreme Court from certain Commonwealth Court final orders, mainly in matters that began in the Commonwealth Court. Otherwise, Superior and Commonwealth Court final orders are reviewable only by allowance.","c":"Pa.R.A.P. 341(d), 1101"}]],["Agency review",[{"t":"Petition for review: 30 days","d":"Review of a quasi-judicial (agency) order, an arbitration award appealable under 42 Pa.C.S. § 763(b), or other orders reviewable by law is by petition for review filed with the appellate prothonotary within 30 days after entry of the order. Cross-petitions are due within 14 days of service of the first petition. Some statutes set 15-day periods (for example, certain Department of Community and Economic Development and procurement-protest determinations).","c":"Pa.R.A.P. 1512(a)-(b)"}]],["Filing, e-filing and confidential information",[{"t":"PACFile","d":"Appellate e-filing is through PACFile, governed by Supreme Court administrative orders. In expedited election matters, the Court has required filings through PACFile whenever counsel has an account. The rule text itself is still labeled interim, so check the current administrative orders for when e-filing is mandatory.","c":"Pa.R.A.P. 125 & cmt.; Order No. 622 J.A. Docket (2024)"},{"t":"Public Access Policy certification","d":"Every appellate filing by an attorney or unrepresented party must include a certification of compliance with the Case Records Public Access Policy, plus a Confidential Information Form or Confidential Document Form where needed. Information such as Social Security and financial account numbers, driver license numbers, and minors’ names and birth dates goes only on the Confidential Information Form. Records sealed below stay sealed on appeal unless the court orders otherwise.","c":"Pa.R.A.P. 127; 204 Pa. Code § 213.81, Policy § 7.0"},{"t":"Mailbox rule for some filings","d":"Petitions for permission to appeal, applications for reargument and briefs sent by first class, express or priority U.S. mail are treated as filed on the mailing date. For petitions and reargument, that date must be shown on a USPS Form 3817 or similar USPS receipt. The notice of appeal is filed in the trial court and is dated when the clerk receives it.","c":"Pa.R.A.P. 1311(b), 2542(b), 2185(a)(1), 905(a)(3)"}]],["Deadlines",[{"t":"Notice of appeal from a final civil order (filed in the trial court)","d":"30 days from entry of the order (civil: the date the clerk notes Rule 236 notice on the docket).\nCannot be extended (Pa.R.A.P. 105(b)); late filing invalidates the appeal (Pa.R.A.P. 902(b)(2)).","c":"Pa.R.A.P. 903(a), 108(b)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the order)","note":"Cannot be extended (Pa.R.A.P. 105(b)); late filing invalidates the appeal (Pa.R.A.P. 902(b)(2))."}},{"t":"Cross-appeal","d":"14 days from service of the first notice of appeal (or the original 30 days, whichever is later).\nIdentify it as a cross-appeal.","c":"Pa.R.A.P. 903(b)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (service of the first notice of appeal)","note":"Identify it as a cross-appeal."}},{"t":"Appeal in Election Code, Local Government Unit Debt Act, or criminal venue/venire matters","d":"10 days from entry of the order.\nThe Supreme Court has shortened Election Code periods by order for specific elections.","c":"Pa.R.A.P. 903(c)(1)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the order)","note":"The Supreme Court has shortened Election Code periods by order for specific elections."}},{"t":"Election that an order sustaining venue or jurisdiction is final","d":"10 days from entry of the order.\nFiled by the party benefiting from the order. The notice of appeal is then due 30 days after the election (Pa.R.A.P. 903(c)(2)).","c":"Pa.R.A.P. 311(b)(1)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the order)","note":"Filed by the party benefiting from the order. The notice of appeal is then due 30 days after the election (Pa.R.A.P. 903(c)(2))."}},{"t":"Motion for post-trial relief (civil)","d":"10 days from verdict, discharge of jury, or nonsuit (jury); notice of nonsuit or filing of decision (non-jury).\nOther parties: 10 days after the first motion is filed. Issues not raised are waived.","c":"Pa.R.Civ.P. 227.1(c)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (verdict, discharge of jury, or nonsuit)","note":"Other parties: 10 days after the first motion is filed. Issues not raised are waived."}},{"t":"Judgment on praecipe if post-trial motions are undecided","d":"120 days from filing of the first post-trial motion.\nA judgment entered this way is final and not subject to reconsideration.","c":"Pa.R.Civ.P. 227.4(1)(b)","due":{"n":120,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the first post-trial motion)","note":"A judgment entered this way is final and not subject to reconsideration."}},{"t":"Application for a Rule 341(c) determination of finality","d":"30 days from entry of the order.\nDeemed denied if the trial court does not act within 30 days of filing. Notice of appeal is due within 30 days of the amended order.","c":"Pa.R.A.P. 341(c)(1)-(2)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the order)","note":"Deemed denied if the trial court does not act within 30 days of filing. Notice of appeal is due within 30 days of the amended order."}},{"t":"Application to amend an interlocutory order to add § 702(b) certification","d":"30 days from entry of the interlocutory order.\nDeemed denied after 30 days without action.","c":"Pa.R.A.P. 1311(b)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the interlocutory order)","note":"Deemed denied after 30 days without action."}},{"t":"Petition for permission to appeal (interlocutory)","d":"30 days from entry of the amended (certified) order, or the denial or deemed denial of certification.\nFiled with the appellate prothonotary. Cannot be extended (Pa.R.A.P. 105(b)).","c":"Pa.R.A.P. 1311(b)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the amended)","note":"Filed with the appellate prothonotary. Cannot be extended (Pa.R.A.P. 105(b))."}},{"t":"Rule 1925(b) Statement of errors","d":"21 days from entry on the docket of the trial judge’s order (minimum; the order sets the actual number of days).\nIssues omitted are waived. Must be filed of record and served on the judge.","c":"Pa.R.A.P. 1925(b)(2)(i), (b)(3)(i)","due":{"n":21,"from":"the trigger date (entry on the docket of the trial judge’s order)","note":"Issues omitted are waived. Must be filed of record and served on the judge."}},{"t":"Transmission of record to appellate court","d":"60 days from filing of the notice of appeal.\nExtended if a Rule 1925(b)(2) extension is granted.","c":"Pa.R.A.P. 1931(a)(1)","due":{"n":60,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the notice of appeal)","note":"Extended if a Rule 1925(b)(2) extension is granted."}},{"t":"Designation of reproduced record","d":"30 days from before the appellant’s brief is due.\nAppellee may counter-designate within 10 days after receiving it.","c":"Pa.R.A.P. 2154(a)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (before the appellant’s brief is due)","note":"Appellee may counter-designate within 10 days after receiving it."}},{"t":"Appellant’s brief","d":"40 days from filing of the record (unless the prothonotary fixes another date).\nChildren’s fast track appeals: 30 days.","c":"Pa.R.A.P. 2185(a)(1)","due":{"n":40,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the record)","note":"Children’s fast track appeals: 30 days."}},{"t":"Appellee’s brief","d":"30 days from service of the appellant’s brief and reproduced record.\nChildren’s fast track appeals: 21 days.","c":"Pa.R.A.P. 2185(a)(1)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (service of the appellant’s brief and reproduced record)","note":"Children’s fast track appeals: 21 days."}},{"t":"Reply brief","d":"14 days from service of the preceding brief.\nMust also be received at least 3 days before argument.","c":"Pa.R.A.P. 2185(a)(1)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (service of the preceding brief)","note":"Must also be received at least 3 days before argument."}},{"t":"Application for reargument","d":"14 days from entry of the appellate judgment or order.\nChildren’s fast track: 7 days. Limit 3,000 words.","c":"Pa.R.A.P. 2542(a)(1)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the appellate judgment or order)","note":"Children’s fast track: 7 days. Limit 3,000 words."}},{"t":"Petition for allowance of appeal to the Supreme Court","d":"30 days from entry of the Superior or Commonwealth Court order (or of the order disposing of a timely reargument application).\nCannot be extended (Pa.R.A.P. 105(b)). Limit 9,000 words.","c":"Pa.R.A.P. 1113(a)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the Superior or Commonwealth Court order)","note":"Cannot be extended (Pa.R.A.P. 105(b)). Limit 9,000 words."}},{"t":"Cross-petition for allowance of appeal","d":"14 days from service of the first petition.\nOr within the original 30 days, whichever is later.","c":"Pa.R.A.P. 1113(b)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (service of the first petition)","note":"Or within the original 30 days, whichever is later."}},{"t":"Answer to petition for allowance of appeal","d":"14 days from service of the petition.\nLimit 9,000 words.","c":"Pa.R.A.P. 1116(a)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (service of the petition)","note":"Limit 9,000 words."}},{"t":"Answer to jurisdictional statement (direct Supreme Court appeal)","d":"14 days from service of the jurisdictional statement.","c":"Pa.R.A.P. 909(b)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (service of the jurisdictional statement)","note":""}},{"t":"Petition for review of agency (quasi-judicial) order","d":"30 days from entry of the order.\nSome statutes set 15 days (Pa.R.A.P. 1512(b)). Cannot be extended.","c":"Pa.R.A.P. 1512(a)(1)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the order)","note":"Some statutes set 15 days (Pa.R.A.P. 1512(b)). Cannot be extended."}},{"t":"Cross-petition for review","d":"14 days from service of the first petition for review.\nOr within the original 30 days, whichever is later.","c":"Pa.R.A.P. 1512(a)(2)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (service of the first petition for review)","note":"Or within the original 30 days, whichever is later."}},{"t":"Anti-SLAPP special motion (UPEPA)","d":"60 days from service of the pleading asserting the claim.\nExtendable for good cause. Effective date of § 8340.16 depends on a Pa. Bulletin notice under Act 72 of 2024 § 3(4); confirm before relying on it.","c":"42 Pa.C.S. § 8340.16(b)","due":{"n":60,"from":"the trigger date (service of the pleading asserting the claim)","note":"Extendable for good cause. Effective date of § 8340.16 depends on a Pa. Bulletin notice under Act 72 of 2024 § 3(4); confirm before relying on it."}}]]],"courts":[],"links":[["Official site","https://www.pacourts.us/"]]},"rulesName":"Pennsylvania Rules of Civil Procedure","rulesSrc":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html","rulesAsof":"as checked November 10, 2026","rulesNote":"Each entry is our summary of the rule, not the rule itself. Tap Read the official text before you rely on it.","rules":[{"n":"51","t":"Title and citation","s":"These rules are the Pennsylvania Rules of Civil Procedure, cited as “Pa.R.Civ.P.”; older citations to “Pa.R.C.P. No.” remain valid. They do not apply in magisterial district courts, which have their own rules (Pa.R.Civ.P.M.D.J.).","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"52","t":"Effective date; application to pending actions","s":"A rule or amendment takes effect on the date the Supreme Court specifies; if none is specified, it takes effect on the first day of July or January following the 30th day after adoption, whichever is earlier. Unless the Supreme Court says otherwise, it applies to actions already pending on the effective date.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"76","t":"Definitions","s":"Defines terms used throughout the rules, including: adult and majority (age 18 or over), minor (under 18), competent adult (18 or older and not a party, nor a party’s employee or relative), and prothonotary (the clerk of the civil court, or the equivalent officer). An affidavit or verified statement may be either sworn before an authorized officer or unsworn and made subject to the penalties of 18 Pa.C.S. § 4904 (unsworn falsification). Other definitions: registered mail includes certified mail; month and year mean calendar month and year; signature includes electronic signatures on court-produced documents; and political subdivision is defined for procedural purposes only.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"101-104","t":"Rescinded","s":"Rules 101, 102, 103 and 104 are rescinded (effective January 1, 2024). Their construction principles now appear in Pa.R.J.A. 104-106; former Rule 104 (numerals) has no replacement.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"105","t":"Bonds","s":"Wherever a rule requires a bond or undertaking with sureties, a bond from an indemnity or surety company authorized to do business in Pennsylvania and approved by the proper authority may be given instead.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"106-108","t":"Rescinded","s":"Rules 106, 107 and 108 (computation of time, including weeks and months) are rescinded (effective January 1, 2024). Time computation is now governed by Pa.R.J.A. 107.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"126","t":"Application and construction of rules","s":"The rules are to be liberally applied to secure the just, speedy, and inexpensive determination of every action, and at any stage the court may disregard a procedural error or defect that does not affect the parties’ substantive rights. The rules are construed under the principles in Pa.R.J.A. 104-115, which replaced former Rules 101-104, 106-108 and 127-153.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"127-153","t":"Rescinded","s":"Rules 127, 128, 129, 130, 131, 132, 133, 151, 152 and 153 are rescinded. Rule 151 was rescinded in 1982; the others were rescinded effective January 1, 2024, and their principles now appear in Pa.R.J.A. 108-115.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"201","t":"Agreements of attorneys","s":"Attorneys’ agreements about the business of the court must be in writing, unless they are made at the bar and noted by the prothonotary in the minutes or by the stenographer in the stenographer’s notes.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"202-204","t":"Rescinded","s":"Rules 202, 203 and 204 are rescinded. Contingent-fee agreements, payments by attorneys, and payments to persons in connection with litigation are now governed by the Rules of Professional Conduct.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"204.1","t":"Format of pleadings and other legal papers","s":"All pleadings, motions and other legal papers must be on good-quality white 8 1/2 by 11 inch paper (dividers excepted), with a 3-inch blank space at the top of the first page for court stamps. Text must be double-spaced (quotations over two lines may be indented and single-spaced), margins at least one inch on all four sides, type no smaller than 12 point, printed on one side only (exhibits may be two-sided), and documents must be firmly bound.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"205","t":"Rescinded","s":"Rule 205, which adopted the ABA Code of Professional Responsibility as the standard of attorney conduct, is rescinded; attorney conduct is governed by the disciplinary rules.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"205.1","t":"Filing legal papers by delivery or mail","s":"A legal paper that needs no judge’s signature or action before filing may be delivered or mailed to the prothonotary, sheriff or other officer with any filing fee; no personal appearance is needed. The attorney’s signature certifies authority to file, the endorsed service address registers the address, and the attorney’s Supreme Court identification number proves the right to practice. Trap: a mailed paper is not filed until the officer actually receives it (there is no mailbox rule).","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"205.2","t":"Filing with the prothonotary; local rules cannot block filing","s":"The prothonotary may not refuse to file a pleading or legal paper that complies with the statewide Rules of Civil Procedure because it fails a local rule requirement, including local format rules (Local Rule 205.2(a)) and local cover-sheet rules (Local Rule 205.2(b)).","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"205.3","t":"Filing originals and copies","s":"A party may file either an original or a copy (including a facsimile copy) of a pleading or legal paper, as long as the copy shows the original was properly signed and, where applicable, verified; the copy is treated as the original. Any other party may demand the original by filing and serving a notice to file it within 14 days of the filing of the notice. The rule does not authorize filing by fax transmission.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"205.4","t":"Electronic filing and service of legal papers","s":"A court may permit or require e-filing by local rule (Local Rule 205.4); papers are filed in PDF or another locally designated format, the e-filed version is the original, and e-filing certifies that a signed (and, where needed, verified) hard copy exists and is subject to Rule 1023.1 sanctions. The filer must keep the signed hard copy for two years after the latest of case disposition, the order resolving the issue, or appellate disposition, and must produce it within 14 days of a notice to produce. The filer bears the risk of transmission failures (though the court may allow nunc pro tunc filing after a good-faith attempt); papers other than original process may be served electronically only if the parties agree or an e-mail address appears on an appearance or prior filing (an e-mail on letterhead is not enough), and e-service is complete when sent.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"205.5","t":"Cover sheet","s":"Whoever starts an action must complete and file the statewide cover sheet designed by the Court Administrator, and the prothonotary may not accept a filing commencing an action without it; the prothonotary must help pro se parties complete it. Family-law actions (protection from abuse, support, custody, divorce, other domestic relations, custody mediation) are excluded, and judicial districts with a qualifying e-filing system under Rules 205.4 and 239.9 are exempt.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"205.6","t":"Confidential information and documents; certification","s":"Anyone filing a document with the prothonotary (the attorney, or the party if unrepresented) must comply with Sections 7.0 and 8.0 of the Case Records Public Access Policy of the Unified Judicial System, unless other authority restricts access. Trap: every filing needs a certification of compliance with the Policy, and, where the Policy requires, confidential information or documents must be submitted on a Confidential Information Form or Confidential Document Form rather than in the public filing.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"206","t":"Rescinded","s":"Rule 206 is rescinded (effective January 1, 1996).","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"206.1","t":"Petition: definition, content, form","s":"A “petition” is an application to strike and/or open a default judgment or judgment of non pros (a judgment against a plaintiff for failure to prosecute), plus any other application a court designates by Local Rule 206.1(a); confessed-judgment relief goes under Rule 2959 and other applications are motions. The petition must specify the relief sought and state the material facts supporting it in consecutively numbered paragraphs with one allegation each. Trap: all grounds for relief, whether to strike or to open, must be raised in a single petition.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"206.2","t":"Answer to petition","s":"An answer must state the material facts constituting the defense, in consecutively numbered paragraphs that correspond to the petition’s paragraphs.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"206.3","t":"Verification of petition or answer","s":"A petition or answer that alleges facts not already of record must be verified (sworn or made subject to the penalties for unsworn falsification).","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"206.4","t":"Rule to show cause","s":"A petition proceeds on a rule to show cause (a court order directing the respondent to answer why relief should not be granted), issued in the court’s discretion under Rule 206.5 unless the court by local rule adopts issuance as of course under Rule 206.6; later procedure follows Rule 206.7. A judgment is stricken without any rule to show cause when a defect on the face of the record is a ground for striking a default judgment. Every court must describe its procedure in Local Rule 206.4(c).","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"206.5","t":"Rule to show cause: discretionary issuance","s":"A petitioner seeking a rule to show cause must attach a proposed order in the prescribed form and notify all other parties that the rule will be requested. If the petition is within Rule 206.1(a), properly pleaded and states prima facie grounds, the court must issue the rule and may stay the proceedings; the order sets the days for an answer, depositions, and argument (or an evidentiary hearing). A default judgment with a defect on the face of the record is stricken without a rule.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"206.6","t":"Rule to show cause: issuance as of course","s":"Where a court has adopted this procedure, a rule to show cause issues automatically when the petition is filed, directing an answer within 20 days after service of the petition on the respondent. The court may stay proceedings, and the petitioner must attach a proposed order in the prescribed form.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"206.7","t":"Procedure after rule to show cause issues","s":"If no answer is filed, the petition’s factual averments may be deemed admitted and the court enters an appropriate order; if the answer raises no disputed material facts, the court decides on petition and answer at the petitioner’s request. If the answer raises disputed material facts, the petitioner may take depositions (or other discovery the court allows) within the time the order sets. Trap: if the petitioner does not, the petition is decided on petition and answer, and the answer’s properly pleaded responsive facts are deemed admitted.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"207","t":"Rescinded","s":"Rule 207 is rescinded (effective January 1, 1996).","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"207.1","t":"Motion to exclude expert testimony relying on novel scientific evidence","s":"A motion to exclude expert testimony based on novel science as inadmissible under Pa.R.E. 702 or 703 must state the expert’s name and credentials, summarize the expected testimony and pinpoint the part to be excluded, give the specific basis and supporting evidence, and attach all relevant CVs and expert reports. No response is needed unless ordered; the court first decides whether to address the issue before trial or defer it to trial, and must require a response if it will decide before trial. A party need not raise expert admissibility before trial unless the court orders it.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"208","t":"Rescinded","s":"Rule 208 is rescinded (effective January 1, 1996).","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"208.1","t":"Motion: definition and scope","s":"A “motion” is any application to the court for an order, but the motion rules (208.1-208.4) do not cover preliminary objections, judgment on the pleadings, summary judgment, requests for special relief (including preliminary injunctions), trial-related motions (nonsuit, jury selection, Rule 207.1 expert motions, motions in limine), post-trial motions, delay-damages motions, petitions, or petitions to open or strike confessed judgments. They also do not apply in asbestos and other specially managed cases, replevin, class actions, family-law actions (Rules 1901-1940.9) or Orphans’ Court proceedings, and do not modify any other rule governing a particular motion.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"208.2","t":"Motion: form and content","s":"A motion must have a caption (court, case number, motion name, moving party), consecutively numbered paragraphs, the material facts and relief sought, a proposed order, and a certificate of service listing each represented party’s attorney, each pro se party, and the service address; it must be signed and endorsed. Verification is not required unless a rule or court order requires it. Local rules may add a statement of authority, an uncontested-motion certification, or a meet-and-confer certification for discovery motions (Local Rules 208.2(c)-(e)).","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"208.3","t":"Motion procedures","s":"By default the court first considers a motion without written responses or briefs, and may not grant relief unless the motion is uncontested or the other parties have had an opportunity for argument (it may deny a defective or untimely motion without argument). A court may instead require responses and/or briefs for motions listed in Local Rule 208.3(b); then an opposing party must respond within 20 days after service of the motion, unless a court order modifies or a local rule enlarges that time. Trap: under Local Rule 208.3(b) a response is required to show opposition even with no factual dispute, and the court may treat an unanswered motion as uncontested.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"208.4","t":"Initial consideration of motion; disputed facts","s":"At first consideration the court may decide the motion or set procedures such as responses, briefs, affidavits, depositions, an evidentiary hearing, or a rule to show cause. When relief depends on disputed facts requiring a record, the court on its own or on request may issue a rule to show cause in the prescribed form, setting days for an answer, depositions, and argument, and Rule 206.7 then governs.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"209","t":"Rescinded","s":"Rule 209 is rescinded (effective January 1, 1996).","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"210","t":"Form of briefs","s":"Briefs must be typewritten, printed or otherwise duplicated and endorsed with the case name, court and number, and the name, address and telephone number of the attorney (or of the party if unrepresented). Courts may add requirements only by Local Rule 210.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"211","t":"Oral argument","s":"Any interested party may request oral argument on a motion, and the court may require it whether or not requested. The court may also decide any motion without oral argument.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"212","t":"Pre-trial conference (renumbered)","s":"Former Rule 212 was renumbered Rule 212.3, effective December 1, 1997; see Rule 212.3.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"212.1","t":"Jury cases: earliest trial date, discovery, pre-trial statement deadlines","s":"In jury cases seeking damages above the compulsory arbitration limit, the court gives notice of the earliest trial date, which should come at least 30 days before the plaintiff’s pre-trial statement is due and may set a discovery deadline. Pre-trial statements are due from the plaintiff at least 60 days, the defendant at least 30 days, and an additional defendant at least 15 days before the earliest trial date. These times may be made earlier by local rule, special order or published trial list, or later by local rule or special order.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"212.2","t":"Jury cases: pre-trial statement content and sanctions","s":"A pre-trial statement must include a brief narrative, the types and amounts of all damages, the names and addresses of all witnesses classified as liability or damages witnesses (“anyone named in discovery” is not enough), all trial exhibits, the expert reports (or Rule 4003.5 interrogatory answers) of every expert, any stipulations, and anything else required locally; listed exhibits must be made available. Trap: if non-compliance causes unfair prejudice, the trial judge must grant relief, which may include barring or limiting undisclosed witnesses, expert opinions not in the submitted report, and unlisted exhibits.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"212.3","t":"Pre-trial conference","s":"At any time the court, on its own or a party’s motion, may call a conference on simplifying issues, a scheduling order, admissions, limiting experts, settlement or mediation, and other matters, and may order an insurance representative with settlement authority to attend. Absent a court order, for a post-pre-trial-statement conference involving settlement, counsel must try in good faith to settle beforehand, a knowledgeable attorney with authority must attend, and an insurance representative with authority must attend or be promptly available by telephone. The resulting order controls the rest of the case unless modified at trial to prevent manifest injustice.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"212.4","t":"Eminent domain valuation experts","s":"In eminent domain cases, the name and statement of valuation of a valuation expert required by Section 703(2) of the Eminent Domain Code must be served within the time for filing a pre-trial statement under Rule 212.1, subject to Rule 212.2(c) sanctions. The statute’s deadline of at least 10 days before trial is suspended to that extent.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"212.5","t":"Settlement conference","s":"At any time the court may, on its own or on motion, schedule a settlement conference (using the Rule 212.6 form), and the attorneys or unrepresented parties must make good-faith efforts to settle beforehand. A knowledgeable attorney with authority must attend, an insurance representative with settlement authority must be present unless the court permits telephone availability, and the court may order others to attend.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"212.6","t":"Settlement conference order form","s":"Provides the form of order scheduling a Rule 212.5 settlement conference, stating the judge, courthouse and time and directing all parties to comply with Rule 212.5(b).","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"213","t":"Consolidation, severance and transfer within a county; wrongful death and survival actions","s":"The court may order joint trials or consolidation of actions in the same county that share a common question of law or fact or arise from the same transaction, and may order separate trials of any claim or issue for convenience or to avoid prejudice (subdivisions (c) and (d) are rescinded). Wrongful death and survival claims may be brought in one action, but if brought separately they must be consolidated for trial (or the later one transferred to the court of the first), and a court may stay one until the other is filed and consolidated or becomes time-barred. An action filed in a court lacking subject-matter jurisdiction is not dismissed but transferred, at the plaintiff’s cost, to a Pennsylvania court with jurisdiction.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"213.1","t":"Coordination of actions in different counties","s":"When actions pending in different counties share a common question of law or fact or arise from the same transaction, any party may, with notice to all parties, move the court where a complaint was first filed to coordinate them; that court may stay the actions and hold a hearing. The court weighs factors such as whether the common question predominates, convenience, delay or prejudice, judicial efficiency, the risk of inconsistent rulings, and the likelihood of settlement, and may stay or transfer proceedings. The coordination order must specify notice and who pays costs, and the final order is certified back to the court where each action began.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"214","t":"Preferences on trial lists","s":"Trial lists give preference to cases in which a new trial has been granted and to cases the court designates on application and cause shown.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"215-215.1","t":"Rescinded","s":"Rules 215 and 215.1 are rescinded; the subject of former Rule 215.1 (Commonwealth Court jury trial cases) is now governed by Pa.R.A.P. 3735.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"216","t":"Grounds for continuance","s":"Grounds for a continuance are: agreement of all parties approved by the court; illness of counsel of record, a material witness or a party (with a physician’s certificate if requested); inability to subpoena or depose a material witness, shown by an affidavit stating the expected testimony, why it is expected, the efforts made, and why the witness will be available later; a special ground allowed by the court; and counsel’s scheduled appearance in attorney disciplinary or judicial discipline proceedings. Except for cause shown, each ground supports only one application per party or aligned group, and a continuance based on a cause known when the trial list was published must be presented at least one week before the trial period begins, with notice to all parties. Absence of a subpoenaed witness justifies a continuance only in limited cases (new facts after service, or a prompt request for attachment); the disciplinary-proceeding grounds are exempt from subdivisions (b)-(e) and Rule 217.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"217","t":"Costs on continuance","s":"If a continuance is requested after the preliminary call of the trial list, the court may make the requesting party pay the opponent’s reasonable costs that an earlier request would have avoided. Trap: while those costs remain unpaid, the party may take no further step in the case without leave of court; a party at fault for the delay cannot recover them even if it wins, and otherwise the costs follow the judgment.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"218","t":"Party not ready when case is called for trial","s":"If the plaintiff is not ready without satisfactory excuse when the case is called, the court may enter a nonsuit on the defendant’s motion or a non pros on its own motion; if the defendant is not ready, the plaintiff may proceed to trial or, on an appeal from compulsory arbitration, ask the court to dismiss the appeal and reinstate the award. Trap: failing to appear for trial is automatically treated as not ready without satisfactory excuse. Relief is by post-trial motion under Rule 227.1 (nonsuit or verdict) or a petition under Rule 3051 (non pros).","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"219","t":"View of premises","s":"A party may ask for a jury view of premises involved in the case before the case is called or during trial; whether to allow it is in the court’s discretion. The court may impose the reasonable costs on the applicant or have them follow the judgment.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"220","t":"Challenge to the array","s":"A challenge to the whole panel (array) of jurors returned for trial must be made in writing and filed on or before the first day of the trial period for which the issues are listed.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"220.1","t":"Preliminary instructions to jurors; electronic devices","s":"Everyone reporting for jury service must be instructed on arrival that, until their service ends, they may not discuss the case (except as the court authorizes), read or listen to news about it, use computers, phones or other communicating devices during trial or deliberations (use during breaks is allowed), or use any device or method to research or disclose information about the parties, witnesses, lawyers, judge, the facts or any related topic. The instructions must be repeated at the start of voir dire, at the start of trial, before deliberations, and as the judge deems appropriate. Jurors must be told to report any violation to the court immediately.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"220.2","t":"Sanctions for violating Rule 220.1","s":"Anyone who violates Rule 220.1’s limits on juror use of electronic devices, or any local or judge-imposed device restriction during court proceedings, may be held in contempt under 42 Pa.C.S. § 4132 et seq. and may face other sanctions, including confiscation of the device.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"220.3","t":"Voir dire of jurors","s":"Voir dire (jury questioning) must be conducted in a judge’s presence unless all parties waive it with the court’s consent, and after the oath the judge instructs prospective jurors on their duties and the Rule 220.1-220.2 sanctions. Voir dire must allow parties to obtain at least 17 categories of information about jurors and their households, including name, year and place of birth, neighborhood and zip code (not street address), education, children, occupations, litigation history, ties to law enforcement, lawyers, the courts or the insurance industry, driving, health, reasons for not serving, ties to the parties, lawyers and witnesses, and ability to refrain from prohibited device use. A written questionnaire may be used but is not enough without an opportunity for oral examination; individual voir dire may be allowed, and voir dire must be recorded in full unless all parties waive (transcribed only on written request or court order).","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"221","t":"Peremptory challenges","s":"Each party gets four peremptory challenges, exercised in turn starting with the plaintiff and then in the order parties were named or joined. To distribute challenges fairly, the court may allow additional challenges and allocate them, or treat multiple plaintiffs, defendants or additional defendants as a single party.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"222","t":"Attorney as witness","s":"If trial counsel is called as a witness for the party the attorney represents, the court may decide whether the attorney may continue as trial counsel for the rest of the trial.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"223","t":"Conduct of trial generally","s":"Subject to due process and the parties’ constitutional rights, the court may make and enforce rules and orders limiting cumulative witnesses, limiting how many attorneys for the same party may actively try the case or examine witnesses, regulating the number and length of addresses, and excluding the public or uninterested persons for good order or morals. Photography and broadcasting are governed by Pa.R.J.A. 1910.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"223.1","t":"Conduct of jury trial","s":"Before evidence is taken, the trial judge must give the Rule 220.1 instructions. The court may also allow jury views, have testimony read back on the jury’s request, charge the jury at any time during trial, give exhibits to the jury for deliberations, and provide a written copy of all or part of the charge for deliberations.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"223.2","t":"Juror note taking","s":"Jurors must be allowed to take notes during evidence, openings and closings (but not during the judge’s legal instructions) and may use them in deliberations, and the court must give a cautionary instruction before openings covering eight specified points (notes are optional, are memory aids not evidence, are confidential, may not be shared until deliberations, stay in the courtroom, and will be destroyed). The court supplies the materials, secures notes at each recess, and destroys them uninspected when the jury is dismissed. Neither court nor counsel may suggest note taking, comment on it, or try to read notes, and juror notes cannot be the basis for a new-trial request.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"223.3","t":"Jury instructions on noneconomic loss in bodily injury or death cases","s":"Where a viable claim for noneconomic loss is made in a bodily injury or death action, the court must give the prescribed instruction covering four items of past and future noneconomic loss: pain and suffering, embarrassment and humiliation, loss of enjoyment of life, and disfigurement. The jury considers eight listed factors, including the plaintiff’s age, injury severity and permanence, effect on daily activities, medical treatment, pain, prior health, and the nature of any disfigurement; the parties or court may modify the instruction to fit the case.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"224","t":"Order of proof","s":"The court may require the plaintiff to present all liability evidence before calling any witness solely on the extent of injury or damages, after which the defendant may move for a nonsuit; if refused, trial continues. The court may allow witnesses out of order.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"225","t":"Opening and closing addresses","s":"Attorneys for each party or group of parties may make an opening address to the jury and an address after the close of testimony; the court may regulate their number, length and order.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"226","t":"Points for charge; directed verdict","s":"Requested jury instructions (points for charge) must be framed so each can be answered by a simple yes or no, and copies must be given to the judge and opposing counsel before closing addresses begin. Trap: a point becomes part of the record only when read into the record or filed with the prothonotary before the post-trial motion; otherwise an appellate court will not review the ruling on it. At the close of all evidence the judge may direct a verdict on any party’s oral or written motion.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"227","t":"Exceptions","s":"Formal exceptions to trial rulings are unnecessary; an exception is deemed taken by the party against whom a ruling is made. Unless the court specially allows otherwise, exceptions to the jury charge must be taken before the jury retires, and on request they are argued outside the jury’s hearing.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"227.1","t":"Post-trial relief","s":"After a jury or non-jury trial, a party seeks a new trial, judgment (including judgment notwithstanding the verdict), removal of a nonsuit, or modification of the decision through a single written Motion for Post-Trial Relief, which replaces the old separate motions and exceptions. It must be filed within 10 days after the verdict, discharge of a deadlocked jury, or nonsuit (jury trial), or after notice of nonsuit or filing of the decision (non-jury trial); once a timely motion is filed, any other party has 10 days after that filing to file its own. Trap: relief is waived unless the ground was raised before or during trial by objection, point for charge, offer of proof or similar means (subject to Pa.R.E. 103(a)) and is specifically stated in the motion with how it was raised; boilerplate is disapproved, and grounds not specified are waived absent leave. The motion must be served promptly on all parties and given to the trial judge; it is not allowed in statutory appeals from local or Commonwealth agency determinations, is required after a trial on appeal from eminent domain viewers, and after an appellate remand is needed only if the remand is for a new trial or the trial court’s order says one is required.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"227.2","t":"Court en banc","s":"The trial judge hears and decides all post-trial motions unless the judge orders a court en banc (a multi-judge panel), which must include the trial judge and may have no more than three judges. If the trial judge cannot act, another judge is designated.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"227.3","t":"Transcript of testimony for post-trial motions","s":"Every post-trial motion must designate the portion of the record to be transcribed; any other party may object within 10 days after the motion is filed and ask for a different, larger or smaller portion. If no portion is designated, transcription is deemed unnecessary, and the trial judge promptly decides any objection.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"227.4","t":"Entry of judgment on praecipe","s":"On a party’s praecipe (a written request to the prothonotary), the prothonotary must enter judgment on a nonsuit, verdict or non-jury decision if no timely post-trial motion is filed, or if timely motions are filed but not all decided within 120 days after the first was filed. Trap: a judgment entered after that 120 days is final as to all parties and issues and cannot be reconsidered, which starts the appeal clock. The prothonotary also enters judgment on praecipe when the court grants or denies relief without entering judgment itself; entry is barred while a delay-damages motion is pending (Rule 238) or in the medical liability situation covered by Rule 1042.72(e)(3).","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"228","t":"Testimony on juror misconduct","s":"Testimony from a juror or anyone else during trial about alleged juror misconduct or jury tampering becomes part of the record, must be taken outside the jury’s hearing, and jurors may be questioned about it.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"229","t":"Discontinuance","s":"A discontinuance is the only way a plaintiff may voluntarily end an action, in whole or in part, before trial begins. Trap: discontinuing as to fewer than all defendants requires the written consent of all parties or leave of court, except that in a professional liability case a plaintiff may discontinue as to a defendant for whom no Rule 1042.3 certificate of merit was filed. The court, on petition and notice, may strike a discontinuance to protect a party from unreasonable inconvenience, harassment, expense or prejudice; court approval is required in cases involving minors, incapacitated persons, wrongful death with a minor beneficiary, and class actions.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"229.1","t":"Late delivery of settlement funds; sanctions","s":"Unless the parties agree otherwise in writing, a settling defendant must deliver settlement funds (not including structured-settlement annuity or future installments) to plaintiff’s counsel, or to an unrepresented plaintiff, within 20 calendar days after receiving the executed release; if court approval is needed, the clock starts upon approval. If payment is late, the plaintiff may seek to invalidate the settlement or file an attorney affidavit with the settlement terms, release, proof the release was delivered more than 20 days earlier, interest-rate certification, proposed order and certificate of service; the defendant has 20 days to respond. If the court finds a violation with no material dispute over the terms, it must award simple interest at the Wall Street Journal prime rate plus one percent from the 21st day until delivery, plus reasonable attorney’s fees for preparing the affidavit.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"229.2","t":"Petition to transfer structured settlement payment rights","s":"A petition to transfer structured settlement payment rights is filed in the county where the payee is domiciled, with the payee and transferee as parties; terms defined in the Structured Settlement Protection Act carry their statutory meaning. The petition, verified by the transferee, must describe the payment provisions and rights being transferred and state in bold the net amount payable to the payee after all fees and the effective annual interest rate, and must attach four items: the Payee’s Affidavit (verified by the payee, covering family, income, support obligations, prior transfers, reasons and debts), a proposed initial order, a transferee’s attorney certification that the transfer complies with the Act and other law, and a proposed final order. If the petition is sufficient the court schedules a hearing (the payee brings two years of tax returns), and within 7 days the transferee must notify the payee, obligor, annuity issuer, the payee’s spouse and anyone receiving child support or alimony from the payee; if denied without hearing, the court states reasons and the payee may amend as of course. The filings are subject to the Public Access Policy under Rule 205.6.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"230","t":"Voluntary nonsuit","s":"A voluntary nonsuit is the only way for a plaintiff to voluntarily end an action, in whole or in part, during trial. It requires leave of court on good cause shown and is not available after the close of all the evidence.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"230.1","t":"Compulsory nonsuit at trial","s":"In a one-plaintiff, one-defendant case, the court on the defendant’s oral motion may enter a nonsuit at the close of the plaintiff’s liability case if the plaintiff has not established a right to relief, considering only the plaintiff’s evidence and any evidence favorable to the plaintiff the defendant introduced before then. With multiple plaintiffs, no nonsuit may be entered until all plaintiffs close; with multiple defendants (including additional defendants), not until all plaintiffs close against all defendants, and then only for all defendants or for a moving defendant if all defendants stipulate on the record that no evidence of that defendant’s liability will be offered. A nonsuit is challenged by a Rule 227.1 post-trial motion.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"230.2","t":"Termination of inactive cases","s":"At least once a year the court must start proceedings to terminate cases with no activity of record for two years or more, serving a notice of proposed termination (electronically under Rule 205.4(g)(1) or under Rule 440) at least 30 days before the proposed termination date. Trap: unless a statement of intention to proceed is filed by that date, the prothonotary enters an order terminating the case for failure to prosecute; a filed statement triggers a status conference and timelines. A petition to reinstate filed within 60 days after the termination order is docketed must be granted; later petitions require timely filing and a reasonable explanation or legitimate excuse for both failures, and a reinstated case that is terminated again is terminated with prejudice, with no further reinstatement.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"231","t":"Second action","s":"After a discontinuance or voluntary nonsuit, the plaintiff may bring a second action on the same cause of action upon paying the costs of the first action. Trap: after a compulsory nonsuit, no second action on the same cause of action is allowed; the remedy is a motion to remove the nonsuit under Rule 227.1.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"232","t":"Counterclaim survives termination of plaintiff’s action","s":"A discontinuance or nonsuit of the plaintiff’s action does not affect the defendant’s right to pursue an already-filed counterclaim. A defendant may end a counterclaim only by discontinuance or voluntary nonsuit, on conditions like those that apply to plaintiffs.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"233","t":"Rescinded","s":"Rule 233 is rescinded (reserved); service of legal papers other than original process is governed by Rules 440 and 441.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"233.1","t":"Frivolous litigation by pro se plaintiff","s":"When a pro se plaintiff sues, a defendant may move to dismiss on the ground that the plaintiff is raising the same or related claims against the same or related defendants that were already resolved by a written settlement or a court proceeding, and the court may stay the action meanwhile. If it dismisses, the court may bar the plaintiff from further pro se suits raising the same or related claims against those defendants without leave of court, and may dismiss on its own any action filed in violation of that bar. The rule does not apply to family law actions or bar a pro se party’s counterclaims in suits the party did not start.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"234","t":"Rescinded","s":"Rule 234 is rescinded; subpoenas are governed by Rules 234.1 et seq.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"234.1","t":"Subpoena to attend and testify","s":"A subpoena is a court order commanding a person to attend and testify at a stated time and place, and it may also require production of documents or things in that person’s possession, custody or control. It may be used only for a trial, hearing or deposition in a pending action, never to compel an ex parte appearance or production before an attorney, party or party representative, and must be served reasonably in advance of the date attendance is required. Rule 4009.21’s 20-day notice requirement does not apply to a deposition subpoena under this rule.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"234.2","t":"Subpoena: issuance, service, fees, prisoners, minors","s":"On a party’s request the prothonotary issues a signed, sealed subpoena otherwise in blank (Rule 234.6 form). Any adult may serve it on an adult in Pennsylvania by personal service under Rule 402(a), by return-receipt restricted-delivery mail, or by ordinary mail with two copies of the Rule 234.9 acknowledgment and a stamped return envelope. One day’s attendance fee plus round-trip mileage must be tendered on demand at service, or enclosed by check with a mailed subpoena; the court may order a jail or prison custodian to produce a confined witness, and a minor witness must be served together with the minor’s guardian unless the court approves otherwise for good cause.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"234.3","t":"Notice to attend; notice to produce","s":"A party may compel another party (or its officer or managing agent) to attend a trial or hearing, and to bring documents or things, by serving a notice to attend in the Rule 234.7 form reasonably in advance; if attendance is not needed, a notice to produce in the Rule 234.8 form compels production. These notices go only to parties, may be served inside or outside Pennsylvania, and are served under Rule 440.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"234.4","t":"Excusing compliance; motion to quash","s":"The party who served a subpoena, notice to attend or notice to produce may excuse compliance. A party, the person served, or anyone with sufficient interest may move to quash, and after a hearing the court may enter an order protecting any party, witness or other person from unreasonable annoyance, embarrassment, oppression, burden or expense.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"234.5","t":"Failure to comply with subpoena or notice","s":"If a witness disobeys a subpoena, the court may issue a bench warrant and, for willful failure, hold the witness in contempt. Trap: a witness served by ordinary mail cannot be subject to a bench warrant or contempt unless the witness returned the signed Rule 234.9 acknowledgment. A party who fails to comply may face Rule 4019(c) sanctions, the opponent’s reasonable expenses including attorney’s fees if the failure was for delay or in bad faith, and contempt after a hearing if willful.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"234.6-234.9","t":"Subpoena and notice forms","s":"Rules 234.6 through 234.9 prescribe the forms for a subpoena to attend and testify (with an optional paragraph for documents and a verified return of service subject to 18 Pa.C.S. § 4904), a notice to attend (naming the officer or managing agent if the party is an entity), a notice to produce, and the notice and acknowledgment of receipt for a subpoena served by ordinary mail. Each form warns of Rule 234.5 sanctions; the subpoena form must be used for any Rule 234.1 subpoena, including for hearings before arbitrators, hearing officers and commissioners.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"235","t":"Notice to Attorney General: constitutionality; charitable bequests or trusts","s":"When an Act of Assembly is challenged as unconstitutional, or a charitable bequest or trust is involved, and the Commonwealth is not a party, the party raising the constitutional question (or the plaintiff in the charity case) must promptly notify the Pennsylvania Attorney General by registered or certified mail, enclose the pleading raising the issue, and file proof of notice. The Attorney General may intervene or be heard without intervening; the court may stay proceedings pending notice and response, or proceed without waiting if circumstances require.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"236","t":"Prothonotary’s notice of orders and judgments","s":"The prothonotary must immediately give written notice of the entry of any order or judgment, with a copy, to each party’s attorney of record (or the party if unrepresented), and for a confessed judgment must mail the defendant notice and copies of the supporting documents, which the plaintiff supplies with a stamped, addressed envelope. The prothonotary notes in the docket that notice was given; the failure to give notice does not affect the judgment’s lien. Notice may be given by fax or other electronic means only if the party has requested it in writing or listed a fax or electronic address on a prior filing (an address on letterhead is not enough). Trap: Rule 236 notice goes to the attorney of record, so the address on the appearance and pleadings (Rules 1012 and 1025) matters.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"237","t":"Notice of praecipe for final judgment","s":"The prothonotary may not accept a praecipe to enter judgment on a nonsuit, jury verdict or non-jury decision unless it certifies that a copy was mailed to every other party who has appeared, or to that party’s attorney of record.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"237.1","t":"10-day notice before judgment of non pros or default judgment","s":"The prothonotary may not enter a judgment of non pros for failure to file a complaint, or a default judgment for failure to plead, by praecipe unless the praecipe certifies that written notice of intent to file it was mailed or delivered after the failure and at least 10 days before the praecipe was filed: for non pros, to the party’s attorney of record or the unrepresented party; for default, to the party against whom judgment will be entered and also to that party’s attorney of record, if any. The 10 days run forward from mailing or delivery under Pa.R.J.A. 107, a copy of the notice must be attached to the praecipe, and the notice and certification cannot be waived. The rule does not apply to judgments entered by court order, on praecipe under a court order, or under a rule to show cause.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"237.2","t":"Agreement to extend time after 10-day notice","s":"After a Rule 237.1 notice is given, the parties may agree in writing, using the Rule 237.6 form signed for both sides, to extend the time to file a complaint, answer or preliminary objections. Trap: if the action is not taken by the agreed date, the prothonotary may enter judgment of non pros or by default without any further notice.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"237.3","t":"Relief from judgment of non pros or default judgment","s":"A petition to open a judgment of non pros or default judgment entered under Rule 237.1 must attach the complaint, preliminary objections and/or answer the petitioner wants to file, and all grounds must be raised in a single petition. If filed within 10 days after the judgment is entered on the docket, the court must open a non pros judgment if the proposed complaint states a meritorious cause of action, and must open a default judgment if any proposed preliminary objection has merit or the proposed answer states a meritorious defense. Trap: a petition filed after those 10 days falls outside the rule and must meet the stricter case-law test, including timeliness and a reasonable excuse for the default.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"237.4-237.6","t":"Forms: 10-day notices and agreement to extend time","s":"Rules 237.4 and 237.5 prescribe the “Important Notice” forms for the Rule 237.1 10-day notice of intent to seek a judgment of non pros (served on the plaintiff or plaintiff’s attorney) or a default judgment (served on the defendant), each warning that judgment may be entered without a hearing unless the recipient acts within ten days from the date of the notice, and listing the lawyer-referral office designated under Rule 1018.1(c). Rule 237.6 prescribes the Rule 237.2 agreement extending the time to file a complaint, answer or preliminary objections, which states that after the agreed date judgment may be entered on praecipe without further notice.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"238","t":"Delay damages in bodily injury, death or property damage cases","s":"On the plaintiff’s request, damages for delay are added to compensatory damages awarded by a jury verdict, non-jury decision or compulsory arbitration award in actions for bodily injury, death or property damage, running from one year after original process was first served until the verdict, decision or award, at the Wall Street Journal prime rate (first edition of each year) plus one percent, simple interest. The period excludes time the plaintiff delayed trial and time after a written defense offer that stayed open at least 90 days or until trial (a sum certain with prompt cash payment, or a fully disclosed structured settlement), if the plaintiff’s recovery did not exceed 125 percent of the offer. Trap: the plaintiff must move within 10 days after the verdict or notice of the decision, with a notice giving the defendant 20 days to answer (unanswered damages are added on praecipe); in arbitration, the plaintiff must give notice at least 20 days before the hearing and the defendant must object at least 10 days before it, using sealed envelopes, and delay damages do not count toward the arbitration jurisdictional limit. The rule does not apply to eminent domain or where delay damages are otherwise allowed; the Explanatory Comment lists yearly prime rates (6 3/4 percent for 2026).","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"239","t":"Local rules","s":"Requirements for adopting and amending local rules of civil procedure are now set out in Pa.R.J.A. 103(d); local rules adopted under former Rule 239 remain effective once compiled and published under Pa.R.J.A. 103(d)(7).","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"239.1-239.7","t":"Required numbering and content of local rules","s":"A court that imposes local requirements must put them in specially numbered local rules: format of papers (Local Rule 205.2(a)), local cover sheets (205.2(b)), applications designated as petitions (206.1(a)), statements of authority, uncontested-motion certifications and discovery meet-and-confer certifications (208.2(c)-(e)), motion responses and briefs (208.3(b), which may treat an unanswered motion as uncontested), and brief requirements (Local Rule 210). Every court must adopt Local Rule 206.4(c) (rule-to-show-cause procedure, including stays of execution pending a petition to open a default judgment), Local Rule 208.3(a) (scheduling, argument and decision of motions, including emergency motions), Local Rule 1028(c) (preliminary objections, except family law and eminent domain), Local Rule 1034(a) (judgment on the pleadings, which may require a response within 20 days) and Local Rule 1035.2(a) (summary judgment). Local rules may not change the statewide content of petitions and answers, Rule 206.7, the 20-day answer to preliminary objections raising fact issues, or the 30-day summary judgment response under Rule 1035.3(a).","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"239.8","t":"Local rules: promulgation, publication, effective date","s":"The requirements for promulgating, publishing and making effective local rules of civil procedure are now in Pa.R.J.A. 103(d), which replaced this rule’s former requirements effective August 1, 2016. Local rules adopted under the former rule remain effective once compiled and published under Pa.R.J.A. 103(d)(7). Rule 239 says the same.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"239.9","t":"Local e-filing rules (Local Rule 205.4)","s":"A court that permits or requires e-filing must adopt a detailed Local Rule 205.4 stating whether e-filing is permissive or mandatory and which actions and papers it covers, the accepted formats (which must include PDF), how non-attorneys access the e-filing website, approved credit and debit cards and whether advance deposits are allowed, payment to any third-party system operator, and the Rule 205.4(f) procedures (filing status messages, electronic or hard-copy files, security, and fees). It may add any other provisions needed to fully describe the system.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"240","t":"Application to waive fees and costs","s":"Requests to waive fees and costs (in forma pauperis status) in civil actions follow Pa.R.J.A. 1990. If the action is begun by writ of summons, the court will not act on the application until a complaint is filed and may deny it if no complaint is filed within 90 days of the application’s filing date. The court may on its own dismiss as frivolous an action filed together with a fee-waiver application, and the filings are subject to the Public Access Policy under Rule 205.6 (this version took effect November 1, 2026).","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"242","t":"Citation of authorities","s":"Authorities are cited as provided in Pa.R.A.P. 126, including its rules on citing non-precedential Superior Court and Commonwealth Court decisions.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"247-247.1","t":"Rescinded","s":"Rules 247 and 247.1 are rescinded; appeals from public-employment arbitration awards are now governed by 42 Pa.C.S. § 933(b).","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"248","t":"Modification of time","s":"Any time period set by a rule of civil procedure may be extended or shortened by written agreement of the parties or by court order.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"249","t":"Authority of individual judge","s":"Except where the court must act en banc, a single judge may perform any function of the court, including interlocutory and ex parte orders, and may do so (other than trying a case) at any time and place within the judicial district. Each court may regulate how business is assigned among its judges.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"250","t":"Scope of chapter","s":"The Chapter 200 rules (Rules 201-250) apply to all civil actions and proceedings.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"400","t":"Who serves original process","s":"Within Pennsylvania, original process (the writ of summons or complaint that starts the case) must be served by the sheriff, except as provided in Rules 400.1 and 1930.4. A competent adult may also serve it in actions seeking an injunction, perpetuation of testimony or a receiver (Rules 1531-1533), partition, declaratory judgment actions seeking only declaratory relief, and actions with complete diversity of citizenship between all plaintiffs and all defendants where at least one defendant is a Pennsylvania citizen. If the sheriff is a party, the coroner serves; service in another county is made by that county’s sheriff, deputized by the sheriff of the county where the action began. Trap: unlike federal practice, private process servers are not generally allowed for in-state service.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"400.1","t":"Service in the First Judicial District (Philadelphia)","s":"In actions begun in Philadelphia, original process may be served in Philadelphia by the sheriff or a competent adult, and in other counties by deputized sheriff service or by a competent adult forwarding the process to that county’s sheriff. In actions begun elsewhere, process may be served in Philadelphia by deputized service or by a competent adult.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"401","t":"Time for service; reissuance, reinstatement, substitution","s":"Original process must be served within Pennsylvania within 30 days after the writ is issued or the complaint is filed (Rule 404 governs out-of-state service). If not served in time, the prothonotary on praecipe reissues the writ or reinstates the complaint, which may be done any number of times, and the new process must be served within 30 days (or the Rule 404 period) after reissuance or reinstatement; a lost writ or complaint may be substituted on praecipe. Trap: a new defendant may be added to a reissued writ or reinstated complaint only if no defendant has yet been served; a complaint filed after a writ may serve as alternative original process. Note also that process stays effective to commence the action only if the plaintiff does not stall it (Lamp v. Heyman, cited at Rule 405).","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"402","t":"Manner of service; acceptance of service","s":"Original process may be served by handing a copy to the defendant, or by handing it to an adult family member at the defendant’s residence (or, if none, the adult in charge there), to the clerk or manager of the hotel, apartment house or other lodging where the defendant lives, or to the defendant’s agent or person in charge at any office or usual place of business. Instead, the defendant or an authorized agent may accept service by filing a separate acceptance-of-service document in the prescribed form.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"403","t":"Service by mail","s":"Where a rule authorizes mail service, process is mailed by any form of mail requiring a receipt signed by the defendant or authorized agent, and service is complete on delivery. If the mail comes back marked refused, the plaintiff may re-mail by ordinary mail with a return address to the same address, and service is complete if it is not returned within 15 days after mailing. Trap: if it comes back marked unclaimed, the plaintiff must serve by another method.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"404","t":"Service outside Pennsylvania","s":"Original process must be served outside Pennsylvania within 90 days of the issuance of the writ, filing of the complaint, or its reissuance or reinstatement. Service may be made by a competent adult as in Rule 402(a), by mail under Rule 403, as provided by the law of the place of service for its courts of general jurisdiction, as provided by treaty, or as directed by a foreign authority in response to a letter rogatory; 42 Pa.C.S. §§ 5323 and 5329(2) provide further alternatives.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"405","t":"Return of service","s":"The person making service must file a return of service with the prothonotary forthwith, and if service is not made and the process is not reissued or reinstated, a return of no service when the service period expires; the return must state the date, time, place and manner of service, who was served, and other facts showing proper service. Proof of mail service requires the signed return receipt or, after a refusal, the refused letter plus an affidavit that the ordinary-mail copy was not returned within 15 days. A non-sheriff must return service by affidavit, and a non-sheriff return of no service must detail the efforts made; no return is needed if the defendant accepted service, and the sheriff notifies the requesting party by ordinary mail.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"410","t":"Service in real property actions","s":"In actions involving title, interest, possession, or liens on real property, the defendant is served under the general rules. In possession or mortgage foreclosure actions, process must also be served on any non-party found in possession; in a possession action that person becomes a defendant (indexed on the plaintiff’s praecipe), but in foreclosure does not become a party. If service is by special order under Rule 430(a), the court directs publication, posting on the most public part of the property, registered mail to the last known address, and/or other methods (see Rule 1064 for quiet-title actions involving subsurface mineral, oil or gas rights).","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"411","t":"Service in support actions","s":"When a domestic relations section official serves original process in a support action within Pennsylvania, the official sends the complaint and order either by ordinary mail, or with one copy by receipt-required mail and one by ordinary mail (complete on return of a receipt personally signed by the defendant or other satisfactory evidence). A defendant who attends the hearing in person is deemed served. The rule applies only when Rule 1910.2 venue is met.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"412","t":"Service by mail in custody, divorce and annulment actions","s":"In custody, partial custody or visitation actions and in divorce or annulment actions, original process may be served within Pennsylvania by mail under Rule 403 as well as by the Rule 402(a) methods.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"420","t":"Service on minors","s":"When the defendant is a minor (under 18), original process is served on the minor as on an adult defendant or by handing a copy to the minor’s guardian. A valid judgment against a minor cannot be obtained until a guardian is appointed.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"421","t":"Service on incapacitated persons","s":"When the defendant is an incapacitated person, original process is served on that person as on a competent defendant, or by handing a copy to the person’s guardian.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"422","t":"Service on the Commonwealth and political subdivisions","s":"Process against the Commonwealth or a Commonwealth officer, department, board, commission or instrumentality must be served by handing a copy to the person in charge at both the defendant’s office and the office of the Attorney General. A political subdivision is served by handing a copy to its authorized agent, the person in charge at its office, or the mayor or the president, chair, secretary or clerk of its tax-levying body (in counties without one, the chair or clerk of the county commissioners). The rule does not apply to appeals from administrative determinations.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"423","t":"Service on partnerships and unincorporated associations","s":"A partnership (and partners named in the action) or an unincorporated association is served on any partner, officer or registered agent, an agent authorized in writing to receive process, or the manager, clerk or person in charge of any regular place of business or activity, provided the person served is not a plaintiff.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"424","t":"Service on corporations and similar entities","s":"A corporation or similar entity is served by handing a copy to an executive officer, partner or trustee, the manager, clerk or person in charge of any regular place of business or activity, or an agent authorized in writing to receive process, provided the person served is not a plaintiff. Trap: residence-type substituted service under Rule 402(a)(2) is not permitted on a corporation.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"425","t":"Service on additional defendants","s":"An additional defendant not already a party is served with original process as if an original defendant, and on request the joining party must furnish copies of all or specified pleadings. A defendant or additional defendant must serve its joinder complaint on every prior party.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"430","t":"Service by special order of court; publication","s":"If service cannot be made under the applicable rule, the plaintiff may move for a special order directing the method of service, with an affidavit describing the investigation into the defendant’s whereabouts and why service cannot be made (a sheriff’s “not found” return alone is insufficient; a good-faith search includes postal inquiries, relatives, neighbors and employers, public records and a reasonable internet search). Publication, when authorized, is made once in the court-designated legal publication and once in a newspaper of general circulation in the county, with the caption, parties, nature of the action and the prescribed notice to defend; publication against unknown heirs or assigns of a named former owner may be allowed generally.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"440","t":"Service of legal papers other than original process","s":"Every paper filed or served in an action (other than original process) must be served on every other party, by handing, mailing or leaving a copy at the attorney of record’s address endorsed on an appearance or prior pleading (or another agreed address), or by fax; if a party has no attorney, service is on the party at the endorsed address, residence or business, or else the last known address. Service by mail is complete on mailing. Fax service is allowed if the parties agree or a fax number appears on an appearance or prior filing, requires a cover sheet with prescribed details, and is complete when transmission is confirmed; e-mail service is governed by Rule 205.4(g).","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"441","t":"Service of other papers on minors or incapacitated persons","s":"Papers other than original process are served, in the Rule 440 manner, on a minor party’s attorney of record, or if none the minor’s guardian, or if neither the minor; and on an incapacitated party’s guardian, guardian ad litem, or attorney of record.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1001","t":"Definition; scope","s":"One “civil action” replaces the old forms of action in assumpsit, trespass and equity, and the procedural distinctions among them are abolished. In Chapter 1000 and Rules 1506 and 1531–1535, “action” means a civil action brought in or appealed to a court subject to these rules. Other forms of action that borrow these rules are styled “civil action—[type],” e.g. “civil action—mandamus.”","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1002","t":"Authority of attorney","s":"Anything this chapter requires or allows a party to do may be done by the party’s attorney, except verification, which must come from the party (see Rule 1024).","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1003","t":"Waiver of rules; extension of time","s":"The parties may waive by agreement the rules on how an action is commenced and the time for serving process or filing and serving pleadings. On cause shown the court may extend or shorten the time to file pleadings or serve process.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1006","t":"Venue; change of venue","s":"An action against an individual may be brought only in a county where the individual may be served, the cause of action arose, a transaction or occurrence giving rise to it took place, venue is authorized by law, or (for equitable relief about property) the property is located; entity defendants follow Rules 2103 (political subdivisions), 2130 (partnerships), 2156 (unincorporated associations) and 2179 (corporations), and in joint or joint-and-several liability cases (except where the Commonwealth is a defendant) venue proper as to one defendant is proper as to all. The court may transfer on petition for the convenience of parties and witnesses (forum non conveniens), or, if a fair and impartial trial cannot be held, the order is certified to the Supreme Court, which picks the new county; the petitioner advances the transfer costs. Trap: improper venue must be raised by preliminary objection or it is waived, and if sustained the case is transferred to a proper Pennsylvania county (plaintiff pays transfer costs) rather than dismissed; the special medical-malpractice venue rule, former Rule 1006(a.1), was rescinded effective January 1, 2023, and the expired reexamination provision was removed by order of February 18, 2026.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1007","t":"Commencement of action","s":"An action may be commenced by filing with the prothonotary (the clerk of the civil court) either a praecipe (a written request to the prothonotary) for a writ of summons, or a complaint. A writ of summons is the court’s formal notice to the defendant that suit has begun, letting the plaintiff file the complaint later; the note warns that a praecipe for a writ may not be used when an unknown defendant is to be identified by a Doe designation, and a cover sheet is required under Rule 205.5.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1007.1","t":"Jury trial; demand; waiver","s":"Where a right to jury trial exists, it is waived unless a party files and serves a written jury demand no later than 20 days after service of the last permissible pleading, by endorsement on a pleading or a separate writing. On an appeal from compulsory arbitration, the right is waived unless the appellant endorses a demand on the appeal or the appellee files and serves a demand within 10 days after being served with the notice of appeal. A demand cannot be withdrawn without the consent of all parties who have appeared, but it is deemed withdrawn if the demanding party, without satisfactory excuse, fails to appear or is not ready when the case is called, in which case any other ready party must demand a jury immediately or waive it.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1008-1011","t":"Rescinded","s":"Rules 1008, 1009 (service), 1010 (reissuance, reinstatement and substitution of writ or complaint) and 1011 (acceptance of service) are rescinded. Service of original process is now governed by Rule 400 et seq., reissuance and reinstatement by Rule 401(b), and acceptance of service by Rule 402(b).","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1012","t":"Entry of appearance; withdrawal of appearance; notice","s":"A party may (but need not) enter a written appearance stating an address for service under Rule 440(a)(1) and a telephone number, and optionally a fax number (listing a fax number is an agreement to accept service by fax); entering an appearance does not waive any defense, including jurisdiction or venue, and all parties must be notified promptly. An attorney may not withdraw without leave of court unless another attorney has already entered or is simultaneously entering an appearance for the party and the change will not delay the case; otherwise the attorney must petition, give the client notice (at the client’s known address, or by mail to the last known address with a certificate detailing efforts to locate the client), file a certificate of service, and promptly mail the client a copy of any order granting withdrawal. The rule supplies forms for a praecipe for entry of appearance, withdrawal without leave, and substitution of counsel.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1012.1","t":"Admission pro hac vice; motion; content","s":"A Pennsylvania-admitted “sponsor” must file a written motion in the action to admit an out-of-state or foreign “candidate” pro hac vice, averring that the information required by Section 81.504 of the IOLTA regulations has been given to the IOLTA Board and either that the fee required by Section 81.505(a) has been paid, attaching the IOLTA Board’s fee payment certification, or averring that the fee is not required under Section 81.505(c); the rule itself does not state the fee amount. Trap: both verified statements must be attached: the candidate’s (every jurisdiction and license number, any discipline or pending disciplinary proceedings, the number of pending Pennsylvania pro hac vice applications and any denials with caption, court and docket number, and agreement to be bound by Pennsylvania law and the Rules of Professional Conduct, to submit to Pennsylvania courts and the Disciplinary Board, and to appoint the sponsor as agent for service) and the sponsor’s (belief after reasonable investigation that the candidate is reputable and competent, the number of cases in which the sponsor is acting as sponsor, and that settlement proceeds will be handled under Rule 1.15 including IOLTA). The sponsor must enter an appearance and remains attorney of record, signing and receiving papers and attending all proceedings unless excused (but not depositions unless ordered); the court must grant the motion unless it finds good cause to deny, and may later revoke admission after a hearing or other meaningful opportunity to respond.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1013","t":"Rescinded","s":"Rule 1013 (sheriff’s return) is rescinded; return of service is governed by Rule 405.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1017","t":"Pleadings allowed","s":"Except in asbestos cases under Rule 1041.1, the only pleadings are a complaint (including a complaint joining an additional defendant) and answer; a reply if the answer contains new matter, a counterclaim or a cross-claim; a counter-reply if a reply to a counterclaim or cross-claim contains new matter; and preliminary objections and a response. No formal joinder of issue is required. Unlike federal practice, preliminary objections are themselves pleadings, and a reply to new matter is part of the pleading sequence; the note says an answer to preliminary objections is needed only when they allege facts not of record.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1018","t":"Caption","s":"Every pleading must carry a caption with the court’s name, the action number and the name of the pleading. The complaint’s caption must state the form of action and the names of all parties, including a Doe designation for an unknown defendant under Rule 2005; later pleadings may name just the first party on each side with an indication of others. Notes: medical professional liability papers must say “Civil Action—Medical Professional Liability Action” (Rule 1042.16), and minors are identified by initials (Rule 2028).","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1018.1","t":"Notice to defend; form","s":"Every complaint, including a complaint against an additional defendant, must begin with a notice to defend substantially in the prescribed form, and no other notice to plead to a complaint is needed. The notice tells the defendant “You have been sued in court” and must act within twenty (20) days after service by entering a written appearance and filing written defenses or objections, warns that judgment may be entered without further notice and that money, property or other rights may be lost, and directs the reader to take the paper to a lawyer or contact the office named for help, including possible reduced-fee or free legal services. Each court designates by local rule the office named in the notice and may require it in additional languages; a defendant served outside the United States gets 60 days to plead under Rule 1026(b).","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1019","t":"Contents of pleadings; general and specific averments","s":"Pennsylvania is a fact-pleading state: the material facts on which a cause of action or defense is based must be stated in concise and summary form, not just notice of the claim. Fraud and mistake must be pleaded with particularity (conditions of mind may be averred generally); conditions precedent may be averred generally but must be denied specifically; time, place and items of special damage must be stated specifically; official documents and judgments may be pleaded by reference. A pleading must say whether an agreement it relies on is oral or written, and a claim or defense based on a writing must attach a copy (or the material part), or state why it is not accessible and give its substance; parts of pleadings and certain county records may be incorporated by reference.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1020","t":"Pleading more than one cause of action; alternative pleading; failure to join; bar","s":"A plaintiff may plead multiple causes of action against the same defendant, each in a separate count with its own demand for relief, and joined plaintiffs must each have separate, headed counts; causes of action and defenses may be pleaded in the alternative. Trap: if one transaction or occurrence gives rise to more than one cause of action formerly in assumpsit and trespass against the same person, they must all be joined in separate counts, and failing to join one waives it against all parties to the action (the comment says there is no mandatory joinder of related equity claims).","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1021","t":"Claim for relief; determination of amount in controversy","s":"A pleading must specify the relief sought, which may be alternative or of several types, including an accounting, but a demand for unliquidated damages may not claim a specific sum. In counties with compulsory arbitration, the plaintiff must state whether the amount claimed does or does not exceed the local arbitration limit, and the court may determine the true amount in controversy and refer the case to arbitration.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1022","t":"Paragraphing","s":"Every pleading must be divided into consecutively numbered paragraphs, each containing, as far as practicable, only one material allegation.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1023","t":"Rescinded","s":"Rule 1023 (former signing and sanctions rule) was rescinded effective July 1, 2002 and replaced by Rules 1023.1–1023.4.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1023.1","t":"Scope; signing of documents; representations to the court; violation","s":"Every pleading, written motion and other paper directed to the court must be signed by at least one attorney of record in the attorney’s own name, or by an unrepresented party; Rules 1023.1–1023.4 do not apply to discovery matters. Signing, filing or later advocating a paper certifies that, after reasonable inquiry, it is not for an improper purpose, its legal contentions are warranted by existing law or a nonfrivolous argument for changing it, its factual allegations have (or, if so identified, likely will have) evidentiary support, and its denials are warranted (or reasonably based on lack of information). After notice and a reasonable opportunity to respond, the court may sanction attorneys, law firms and parties responsible for a violation; this signature rule does not replace party verification under Rule 1024, and 42 Pa.C.S. § 8355 is suspended.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1023.2","t":"Motion for sanctions","s":"A sanctions motion must be separate from other requests and describe the specific conduct claimed to violate Rule 1023.1(c). Trap (safe harbor): it may not be filed unless it certifies, and attaches, a written notice and demand served on the signer that identifies each offending portion with specificity, explains why, and demands withdrawal or correction, and the paper was not withdrawn or corrected within 28 days after service of the demand. The motion must be filed in the trial court before final judgment, and the prevailing party may be awarded reasonable expenses and attorney’s fees for the motion.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1023.3","t":"Sanctions upon rule to show cause","s":"On its own initiative, the court may enter an order describing conduct that appears to violate Rule 1023.1(c) and directing an attorney, law firm or party to show cause why it has not violated the rule.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1023.4","t":"Sanctions","s":"A sanction must be limited to what suffices to deter repetition by the offender or others similarly situated, and may include nonmonetary directives (such as striking the offending document), a penalty paid into court, or, if imposed on motion and warranted for deterrence, payment of some or all of the movant’s reasonable attorneys’ fees and expenses directly caused by the violation; except in exceptional circumstances a law firm is jointly responsible for its partners, associates and employees. Monetary sanctions may not be imposed on a represented party for unwarranted legal contentions, and the court may not impose monetary sanctions on its own initiative unless it issued the show-cause order before a voluntary dismissal or settlement. The court must describe the violation and explain the basis for the sanction.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1024","t":"Verification","s":"Every pleading containing an averment of fact not of record or a denial of fact must state that it is true on the signer’s personal knowledge or information and belief and must be verified; inconsistent factual averments require a statement that the signer cannot determine after reasonable investigation which is true but believes one is. Unlike the federal rules, verification must be made by one or more of the parties themselves, not counsel, unless all parties lack sufficient knowledge or are outside the jurisdiction and cannot verify in time, in which case any person with sufficient knowledge may verify, stating the source of the information and why a party did not verify.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1025","t":"Endorsement","s":"Every pleading or legal paper must be endorsed with the name of the party’s attorney (or of the party, if unrepresented), an address where papers may be served under Rule 440(a), and a telephone number; a fax number may be added, and listing one is an agreement to accept service by fax.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1026","t":"Time for filing; notice to plead","s":"Every pleading after the complaint must be filed within 20 days after service of the preceding pleading, but no responsive pleading is required unless the preceding pleading contains a notice to defend or is endorsed with a notice to plead. A defendant served outside the United States has 60 days from service of the complaint to plead, and professional liability defendants follow Rule 1042.4; extra time may be sought under Rule 248.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1027","t":"Rescinded","s":"Rule 1027 (service of copies) is rescinded; service of pleadings and other legal papers is governed by Rule 440.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1028","t":"Preliminary objections","s":"Instead of federal-style Rule 12 motions, any party may file preliminary objections to any pleading, limited to: (1) lack of subject-matter or personal jurisdiction, improper venue, or improper form or service of a writ or complaint; (2) failure to conform to law or rule of court, or scandalous or impertinent matter; (3) insufficient specificity; (4) legal insufficiency (demurrer); (5) lack of capacity to sue, nonjoinder of a necessary party or misjoinder of a cause of action; (6) a prior pending action or an agreement for alternative dispute resolution; (7) failure to exercise or exhaust a statutory remedy; and (8) an adequate non-statutory remedy at law. Traps: all preliminary objections must be raised at one time; statute of limitations and statute of frauds may be raised only as new matter, not by preliminary objection; and objections under (1), (5), (6), (7) or (8) that depend on facts not of record must be endorsed with a notice to plead or no response is required. The opposing party may file an amended pleading as of course within 20 days after service of the objections (mooting them); if objections are overruled the objector may plead over within 20 days after notice of the order, any allowed or required amendment is due within 20 days after notice of the order (unless the court sets another time), and an amended pleading is attacked by new preliminary objections.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1029","t":"Denials; effect of failure to deny","s":"A responsive pleading must admit or deny each averment of fact, paragraph by paragraph, specifying what part of a partly denied averment is admitted. Trap: averments requiring a response are admitted unless denied specifically or by necessary implication, and a general denial or demand for proof counts as an admission; a statement that after reasonable investigation the party lacks knowledge sufficient to form a belief counts as a denial, but not where the pleader must know the truth. Averments to which no response is required are deemed denied; in actions for bodily injury, death or property damage (those subject to delay damages under Rule 238), averments may be denied generally except those about who committed a material act, agency or employment, ownership, possession or control of the property or instrumentality, averments supporting other relief, and averments in preliminary objections, which must be denied specifically.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1030","t":"New matter","s":"All affirmative defenses, including accord and satisfaction, arbitration and award, consent, discharge in bankruptcy, duress, estoppel, failure of consideration, fair comment, fraud, illegality, immunity from suit, impossibility, justification, laches, license, payment, privilege, release, res judicata, statute of frauds, statute of limitations, truth and waiver, must be pleaded in the responsive pleading under the heading “New Matter,” along with any other material facts that are not mere denials. Assumption of the risk, comparative negligence and contributory negligence need not be pleaded and, if pleaded, are deemed denied without a reply. Because new matter triggers a reply under Rule 1017, failing to answer properly endorsed new matter can result in admissions under Rule 1029.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1031","t":"Counterclaim","s":"In the answer, under the heading “Counterclaim,” the defendant may assert any cause of action cognizable in a civil action that the defendant has against the plaintiff when the answer is filed. A counterclaim need not diminish or defeat the plaintiff’s claim and may seek relief greater in amount or different in kind.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1031.1","t":"Cross-claim","s":"Any party may plead, in an answer or reply under the heading “Cross-claim,” a cause of action against any other party asserting that the other party is solely liable on the underlying cause of action, or is liable to or with the cross-claimant on any cause of action arising out of the same transaction or occurrence (or series) as the underlying claim. The note says this covers liability-over and joint-and-several claims, and that cross-claims in class actions are limited by Rule 1706.1.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1032","t":"Waiver of defenses; exceptions; suggestion of lack of subject-matter jurisdiction or failure to join indispensable party","s":"A party waives every defense and objection not raised by preliminary objection, answer or reply, except defenses that need not be pleaded under Rule 1030(b), failure to state a claim, failure to join an indispensable party, failure to state a legal defense, failure to exercise or exhaust a statutory remedy, an adequate remedy at law, and other nonwaivable defenses. Whenever it appears the court lacks subject-matter jurisdiction or an indispensable party is missing, the court must transfer the case to a Pennsylvania court with jurisdiction or order joinder, and dismiss only if neither is possible.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1033","t":"Amendment","s":"With the adverse party’s filed consent or leave of court, a party may at any time change the form of action, add a party, correct a party’s name, or otherwise amend, including to plead new events or new causes of action or to conform to the evidence. A name correction relates back if, within 90 days after the limitations period ran, the correct party received notice so as not to be prejudiced and knew or should have known it would have been sued but for the mistake; substituting a real name for a Doe designation relates back if the defendant had actual or constructive notice within the Rule 401 service period and knew or should have known it would have been named but for lack of knowledge of its name. A motion to amend must attach a clean copy and a redline comparison copy (strikethrough deletions, underlined additions), and the clean copy controls if they differ.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1034","t":"Motion for judgment on the pleadings","s":"After the relevant pleadings are closed (only those between the parties to the motion), but early enough not to unreasonably delay trial, any party may move for judgment on the pleadings, and the court enters whatever judgment or order the pleadings warrant. Each court must have a local rule (Local Rule 1034(a)) on the procedure.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1035","t":"Rescinded","s":"Former Rule 1035 (summary judgment) was rescinded effective July 1, 1996 and replaced by Rules 1035.1–1035.5. Its note says that in asbestos litigation a summary judgment motion by one defendant on a ground common to others is deemed filed for all of them.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1035.1","t":"Motion for summary judgment; definition","s":"For summary judgment purposes, the “record” includes the pleadings; depositions, answers to interrogatories, admissions and affidavits; and expert reports signed by the expert that would comply with Rule 4003.5(a)(1) if filed, whether or not they were produced in discovery.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1035.2","t":"Motion","s":"After the relevant pleadings close, but without unreasonably delaying trial, any party may move for summary judgment in whole or in part when (1) there is no genuine issue of material fact as to a necessary element of the claim or defense that could be established by more discovery or an expert report, or (2) after completion of relevant discovery, including expert reports, the adverse party who bears the burden of proof at trial has failed to produce evidence of facts essential to the claim or defense that would require submission to a jury. Subdivision (2) is a “no evidence” style motion available to the party without the burden of proof; the note adds that, under the Nanty-Glo rule, the movant’s own oral testimony, even uncontradicted, generally cannot establish the absence of a fact issue. Partial summary judgment is interlocutory, and each court must have a local rule (Local Rule 1035.2(a)) on the procedure.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1035.3","t":"Response; judgment for failure to respond","s":"Trap: the adverse party may not rest on the pleadings and must file a response within 30 days after service of the motion, identifying either fact issues arising from record evidence that contradicts the movant’s evidence or from a challenge to the credibility of the movant’s witnesses, or record evidence establishing the essential facts the motion says are missing. The respondent may supplement the record or explain why it cannot yet present the needed evidence, the court may permit further affidavits or discovery, and summary judgment may be entered against a party who does not respond. The court may also rule before trial without written responses or briefs if no party is prejudiced (that is, each had a full and fair chance to supplement the record and oppose), but must then state its reasons in a written opinion or on the record.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1035.4","t":"Affidavits","s":"Supporting and opposing affidavits must be made on personal knowledge, set out facts that would be admissible in evidence, and show that the signer is competent to testify to them. Verified or certified copies of papers referred to must be attached or served with the affidavit, and the court may allow affidavits to be supplemented or opposed by depositions, interrogatory answers or further affidavits.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1035.5","t":"Procedure when judgment is denied or not rendered on the whole case","s":"If summary judgment is denied or granted only in part and trial is needed, the court may, if practicable, determine which material facts are undisputed and which are actually controverted, and enter an order specifying the undisputed facts, including any amount of damages not in controversy. Those facts are deemed established at trial.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1036","t":"Dismissal upon affidavit of noninvolvement","s":"In actions where a statute allows dismissal on an affidavit of noninvolvement (the note cites claims against construction design professionals under 42 Pa.C.S. § 7502 and health care providers under Section 506 of the MCARE Act), a party seeking dismissal files a motion with the affidavit attached, and any opponent may respond. If the court finds a prima facie case for dismissal, it must allow the opponent limited discovery solely on involvement and the chance to file evidence, then schedule argument limited to whether that evidence, viewed most favorably to the opponent, would require the involvement issue to go to a jury.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1036.1","t":"Reinstatement of claim dismissed upon affidavit of noninvolvement","s":"Any party may move to reinstate a party dismissed under Rule 1036 by setting forth facts showing the affidavit of noninvolvement was false or inaccurate, and any party may respond. If the court finds a prima facie case of involvement, it must allow limited discovery solely on involvement and the filing of evidence, then hold argument limited to whether the movant’s evidence, viewed most favorably to the movant, would require the involvement issue to go to a jury.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1037","t":"Judgment upon default or admission; assessment of damages","s":"If an action began by writ of summons, the defendant may have the prothonotary enter a rule to file a complaint, and if none is filed within 20 days after service of the rule, a judgment of non pros (dismissal for the plaintiff’s failure to prosecute) on praecipe; on the plaintiff’s praecipe, the prothonotary enters default judgment for failure to plead in time to a complaint containing a notice to defend, or for relief admitted in the defendant’s pleadings. Trap: both praecipes require the Rule 237.1 certification of prior written notice of intent. The prothonotary assesses damages for a sum certain; otherwise damages are tried with issues limited to amount, and where only repair costs are claimed the plaintiff may have damages assessed on an itemized repair affidavit mailed to the defendant by registered mail with notice of an assessment date at least 10 days after mailing, unless the defendant files a praecipe for trial; only the court may grant equitable relief on a default.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1038","t":"Trial without jury","s":"A nonjury trial is conducted as nearly as possible like a jury trial, with the same rights, including the right to move for nonsuit. The judge’s decision may consist of general findings but must dispose of all claims, may include specific findings and conclusions, and may be given orally (then transcribed and filed) or in writing; the prothonotary notifies all parties of the filing date. The decision must be rendered within 7 days after trial ends, except in protracted or extraordinarily complex cases; post-trial relief follows Rule 227.1.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1038.1","t":"Case submitted on stipulated facts","s":"A case may be submitted to a judge without a jury on stipulated facts, following the nonjury trial rules as far as practicable.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1038.2","t":"Abolition of case stated","s":"The common law “case stated” procedure is abolished; parties use submission on stipulated facts under Rule 1038.1 instead.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1038.3","t":"Equitable relief; advisory verdict by jury","s":"In any case with a claim for equitable relief, the court on its own motion or a party’s petition may submit any or all fact issues on that claim to a jury. The jury’s verdict is advisory only, in the form of answers to specific questions, and does not bind the court or create a jury right that did not previously exist.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1039","t":"Rescinded","s":"Rule 1039 (entry of judgment on praecipe) is rescinded; see Rule 227.4.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1040","t":"Suspension of Acts of Assembly","s":"All statutes inconsistent with these rules are suspended to the extent of the inconsistency.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1041","t":"Rescinded","s":"Rule 1041 (former action in trespass) is rescinded.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1041.1","t":"Asbestos litigation; special provisions","s":"In any action alleging injury or death from asbestos exposure (including added silica claims), the general civil rules apply with special changes: papers must be captioned “Civil Action—Asbestos,” and within 20 days after service of the complaint the defendant must enter an appearance, which itself operates as a denial of all factual averments, an assertion of all affirmative defenses, and a claim for indemnity and contribution against every other party. No further pleadings are allowed after the complaint except preliminary objections under Rule 1028(a)(1), (2), (5) and (6) and complaints joining additional defendants; joinder after the 60-day period of Rule 2253 needs no leave of court (though the joined party may object), and one defendant’s summary judgment motion on a common ground is deemed filed for all such defendants.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1041.2","t":"Civil action; alternative procedure","s":"In multi-party actions such as mass torts, product liability or toxic substance cases, the court on motion or its own initiative may order the parties to follow the asbestos procedure of Rule 1041.1(a) and (c)–(f). If many cases are affected, the court may require a special caption designation and a separate docket or docketing code.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1042","t":"Rescinded","s":"Rule 1042 is rescinded.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1042.1","t":"Professional liability actions; scope; definition","s":"These rules govern civil actions in which a patient or client asserts a professional liability claim, including lack of informed consent, against a licensed professional or an entity responsible for one. “Licensed professional” means a health care provider as defined in Section 503 of the MCARE Act, an accountant, architect, chiropractor, dentist, engineer or land surveyor, nurse, optometrist, pharmacist, physical therapist, psychologist or veterinarian licensed in Pennsylvania, an attorney at law, or any of these licensed in another state.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1042.2","t":"Complaint","s":"A complaint must identify each defendant against whom a professional liability claim is asserted (the note suggests stating that the defendant is a licensed professional with offices in a named county and that a professional liability claim is asserted). A defendant may raise noncompliance by preliminary objection, but doing so is not a prerequisite to a Rule 1042.6 notice of intent to enter non pros.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1042.3","t":"Certificate of merit","s":"In any claim that a licensed professional deviated from an acceptable professional standard (including lack of informed consent), the plaintiff’s attorney, or an unrepresented plaintiff, must file with the complaint or within 60 days after filing it a signed certificate of merit stating that (1) an appropriate licensed professional has given a written statement that there is a reasonable probability the defendant’s care, skill or knowledge fell outside acceptable professional standards and caused the harm, (2) the claim rests solely on the deviation of other licensed professionals for whom the defendant is responsible, or (3) expert testimony is unnecessary. A separate certificate is needed for each licensed professional defendant (a single certificate may cover both (1) and (2) claims against one defendant), a defendant’s professional liability counterclaim needs one, and a joinder or cross-claim needs one only if based on unrelated negligence; trap: certifying under (3) generally bars the plaintiff from later using expert testimony on standard of care and causation. On good cause the court must extend the time by up to 60 days per order, on a motion filed by the 30th day after a Rule 1042.6(a) notice of intent or before an earlier extension expires, whichever is later, and the motion tolls the deadline; a certificate not signed by an attorney must attach the professional’s written statement, or the defendant may proceed under Rule 1042.11.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1042.4","t":"Responsive pleading","s":"A professional liability defendant must file a responsive pleading within the time set by Rule 1026 or within 20 days after service of the certificate of merit on that defendant, whichever is later.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1042.5","t":"Discovery","s":"Before filing a certificate of merit, a professional liability plaintiff may seek only production of documents and things and entry on property, and needs leave of court for any other discovery on that claim. The note says the court must allow discovery needed for a licensed professional to determine whether there was a deviation, and defendants may still seek a protective order under Rule 4012.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1042.6","t":"Notice of intent to enter judgment of non pros for failure to file certificate of merit; motion to determine necessity; form of notice","s":"Trap: a defendant wanting a non pros under Rule 1042.7 must first file and serve a written notice of intention on the plaintiff’s attorney (or the unrepresented plaintiff) no sooner than the 31st day after the complaint was filed, warning that judgment will be entered after 30 days if no certificate is filed; no notice is needed if the court already extended the deadline and it passed, or denied an extension. After a notice, the plaintiff may move for a ruling on whether a certificate is required; the motion tolls the deadline, and if a certificate is required it is due within 20 days after the order is docketed or the original period, whichever is later. The note warns that once non pros is entered, the plaintiff can no longer argue that no certificate was needed.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1042.7","t":"Entry of judgment of non pros for failure to file certification; form of praecipe","s":"On the defendant’s praecipe, the prothonotary must enter judgment of non pros for failure to file a timely certificate of merit only if no motion to determine necessity or timely extension motion is pending, no certificate has been filed, and (unless notice was excused under Rule 1042.6(b)) a certificate of service of the notice of intention is attached and the praecipe is filed at least 30 days after the notice was filed. The prothonotary may not enter judgment if a certificate was filed before the praecipe, Rule 237.1 notice does not apply, and the rule supplies a praecipe form covering all claims or listed claims.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1042.8","t":"Motion to strike; defect of certificate of merit","s":"If the court strikes a claim because the certificates of merit did not comply with Rule 1042.3(b) (for example, no separate certificate per defendant), it must give the plaintiff 20 days to file a certificate that cures the defect.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1042.9","t":"Sanctions","s":"If a defendant covered by a certificate of merit is dismissed by voluntary dismissal, verdict or court order, the plaintiff must give that defendant the supporting licensed professional’s written statement within 30 days of the defendant’s written request, or within 30 days after resolution of all claims against other licensed professionals if those remain pending. The court may impose sanctions, including those in Rule 1023.4, on an attorney who improperly certifies that a supporting written statement was obtained.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1042.10","t":"Certificate of merit; form","s":"The Rule 1042.3(a) certificate must be substantially in the prescribed form, captioned as to a named defendant and signed and dated by the attorney or party, checking one or more options: an appropriate licensed professional has supplied a written statement that the defendant’s care fell outside acceptable standards and caused the harm; and/or the claim rests solely on others for whom the defendant is responsible and a professional has supplied such a statement as to them; or expert testimony is unnecessary.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1042.11","t":"Notice of intent to enter non pros for failure to file a written statement from an appropriate licensed professional; form","s":"When a certificate of merit signed by a party rather than an attorney lacks the licensed professional’s written statement required by Rule 1042.3(e), a defendant seeking non pros under Rule 1042.12 must first file a notice of intent in the prescribed form, warning that judgment of non pros will be entered after 30 days from the filing of the notice if the written statement is not filed, and identifying the claims affected.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1042.12","t":"Entry of judgment of non pros for failure to file a written statement; form of praecipe","s":"On the defendant’s praecipe, the prothonotary must enter judgment of non pros for failure to file the Rule 1042.3(e) written statement if none has been filed, a certificate of service of the Rule 1042.11 notice is attached, and the praecipe is filed at least 30 days after the notice was filed. Judgment may not be entered if the statement was filed before the praecipe, Rule 237.1 does not apply, and the rule supplies the praecipe form.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1042.16","t":"Medical professional liability actions; legal papers; caption","s":"In any action with a medical professional liability claim, the caption of all legal papers (or the cover sheet, where a county requires one) must state “Civil Action—Medical Professional Liability Action.”","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1042.21","t":"Medical professional liability actions; motion for settlement conference or mediation","s":"Before expert reports are exchanged, a health care provider may move for a settlement conference or court-ordered mediation; if not all parties consent, the movant must certify a belief that settlement is realistically possible, and a movant seeking mediation must describe it and pay for it. The court must consider any objection before ruling.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1042.26","t":"Medical professional liability actions; expert reports","s":"Rules 1042.26–1042.38 govern pretrial production of expert reports in medical professional liability actions against health care providers. They apply only where the court has not set case management deadlines for expert reports by order or otherwise.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1042.27","t":"Requests for production of expert reports; responses; general provisions","s":"Parties may request expert reports under Rules 1042.28–1042.30, naming the requesting party and the party to whom the request is directed. Reports must cover all liability issues, including negligence and causation, on which the responding party will offer expert testimony against the requesting party, reflect the best information then available, be signed by the expert, and comply with Rule 4003.5; requests may be filed with the court but the reports are discovery material not filed except under Rule 4002.1.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1042.28","t":"Defendant’s request to plaintiff for production of expert reports; response","s":"A defendant facing a professional negligence claim may serve a request on the plaintiff (in the Rule 1042.36 form) no earlier than 90 days after filing its original answer. The plaintiff must furnish expert reports supporting the claims against that defendant within 180 days after service of the request.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1042.29","t":"Plaintiff’s request to defendant or additional defendant for production of expert reports; response","s":"A plaintiff who has furnished expert reports to a defendant may serve a request (in the Rule 1042.37 form) for that defendant’s defense expert reports: if the plaintiff’s reports answered a Rule 1042.28 request, the defendant must respond within 60 days after service; if the plaintiff produced reports unrequested, the request may be served only after 90 days following the filing of the certificate of merit as to that defendant, and the defendant has 120 days to respond. A responding defendant that has joined other parties must also cover its expert testimony on those claims.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1042.30","t":"Defendant’s or additional defendant’s request to another defendant for production of expert reports; response","s":"A defendant or additional defendant that has furnished expert reports supporting its claims against other parties may serve a request (in the Rule 1042.38 form) on another defendant, but not earlier than 90 days after a certificate of merit was served on that party. The recipient must furnish expert reports supporting its defenses and its claims against the requester and other joined parties within 60 days after service.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1042.31","t":"Failure to produce report; sanctions; summary judgment","s":"A party not receiving a required expert report may move to compel; the court considers case complexity, diligence and other factors and must give a diligent party reasonable time to complete discovery and produce a report (the note says incomplete discovery or unidentified experts alone is no excuse). Violating an order compelling production may be sanctioned, including by barring expert testimony, and the court must promptly consider a summary judgment motion based solely on such a preclusion order.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1042.32","t":"Additional and supplemental reports","s":"Until a court-set expert deadline passes, and unless the court has precluded it, a party may serve additional or supplemental expert reports without leave of court, which may raise new liability or causation theories or new defenses and may come from different experts.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1042.36-1042.38","t":"Expert report request forms","s":"These rules set the required forms for expert report requests in medical professional liability actions: Rule 1042.36 (defendant to plaintiff, requesting reports within one hundred eighty (180) days of service), Rule 1042.37 (plaintiff to defendant or additional defendant, requesting reports within sixty (60) days under Rule 1042.29(a)(2) or one hundred twenty (120) days under Rule 1042.29(a)(3), deleting the inapplicable paragraph), and Rule 1042.38 (defendant or additional defendant to another, requesting reports within sixty (60) days). Each form states that copies of all expert reports must be served on all other parties, and the latter two require reports to cover claims against joined parties.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1042.41","t":"Medical professional liability actions; scheduling order","s":"One year after the first answer is filed in a medical professional liability action, any party may move for a scheduling order, and the court must within 30 days issue one, covering completion of discovery and production of expert reports, or schedule a case management conference. The rule does not apply where the court has already set such schedules.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1042.51","t":"Medical professional liability actions; motion for pre-trial conference; mediation; report of cases not tried","s":"After liability expert reports have been produced (or when the motion states all parties have exchanged them), any party may move for a pretrial conference, which must be scheduled within 60 days of the motion under Rule 212.3; at it the court sets another conference or a trial or tentative trial date and asks whether the parties will mediate. On February 1 and September 1 each year, each court administrator must report to the Administrative Office of Pennsylvania Courts all medical professional liability cases not tried within nine months of such a conference; the rule does not apply once a trial date is set.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1042.71","t":"Medical professional liability actions; damages; findings","s":"At any party’s request, the trier of fact (jury or judge) must make separate findings for each plaintiff of past damages for medical and related expenses, lost earnings and noneconomic loss, each in a lump sum (subject to Section 508 of the MCARE Act on collateral sources), and of future damages for medical and related expenses by year, and for loss of earnings or earning capacity and noneconomic loss each in a lump sum.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1042.72, 1043-1048","t":"Rescinded","s":"Rules 1042.72, 1043, 1044, 1045, 1046, 1047 and 1048 are rescinded.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1051","t":"Ejectment; conformity to civil action","s":"Except as this subchapter provides otherwise, an action in ejectment (to recover possession of land) follows the civil action rules.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1052","t":"Ejectment; venue","s":"An ejectment action may be brought only in a county where the land, or part of it, is located.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1053","t":"Rescinded","s":"Rule 1053 (service in ejectment) is rescinded; service in actions involving real property is governed by Rule 410.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1054","t":"Ejectment; specific averments; abstract of title","s":"The plaintiff must describe the land in the complaint, and each party must set out in the complaint or answer an abstract of the title it relies on, going back at least to the common source of the parties’ competing titles.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1055","t":"Ejectment; pleading more than one cause of action","s":"The ejectment plaintiff may join claims for rents, profits or any other damages arising from the defendant’s possession of the land, such as delinquent rent or installment-contract payments.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1056","t":"Ejectment; counterclaim; conditional verdict","s":"The defendant may counterclaim on any claim arising from the same transaction or occurrence (or series) as the plaintiff’s cause of action, whether or not the plaintiff seeks damages, and a conditional verdict may be entered in an appropriate case.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1057","t":"Ejectment; judgment; execution","s":"An ejectment judgment is enforced under Rules 3160 through 3165.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1058","t":"Ejectment; trial without jury","s":"A nonjury ejectment trial follows Rule 1038.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1061","t":"Quiet title; conformity to civil action; scope","s":"An action to quiet title follows the civil action rules through judgment, but the rule confers no right to a jury trial. It may be brought to compel an adverse party to bring ejectment; where ejectment will not lie, to determine any right, lien, title or interest in land or the validity or discharge of a document affecting it; to compel an adverse party to record, cancel, surrender, satisfy or admit the validity, invalidity or discharge of such a document; or to obtain possession of land sold at a judicial or tax sale.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1062","t":"Quiet title; venue","s":"A quiet title action may be brought only in a county where the land, or part of it, is located.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1063","t":"Quiet title; commencement of action","s":"A quiet title action must be commenced by filing a complaint with the prothonotary, not by writ of summons.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1064","t":"Quiet title; service","s":"In quiet title actions involving subsurface mineral, oil or natural gas rights, a plaintiff serving by publication under Rule 430 who learns of a last known address of the defendant outside the county where the property lies must describe the search for the defendant in that locale in the Rule 430(a) affidavit. Service otherwise follows Rule 410 for actions involving real property.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1065","t":"Quiet title; specific averments","s":"The plaintiff must describe the land in the complaint. For subsurface mineral, oil or natural gas rights, the complaint must attach a summary of the abstract of that title (or the full abstract if the title documents are not in the courthouse records) and a statement of acreage with a metes and bounds description if available, or another description sufficient to identify the land.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1065.1","t":"Quiet title; notice; claim for adverse possession","s":"In a quiet title action under 42 Pa.C.S. § 5527.1 by a possessor claiming title by adverse possession of not less than ten years, the plaintiff must give the record owner (and heirs, successors and assigns) the prescribed notice identifying the property by address, deed reference, parcel number and metes and bounds description. The notice tells the owner that to challenge the claim, the owner must respond within one year after the complaint and notice are served by commencing an action in ejectment against the plaintiff.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1066","t":"Quiet title; form of judgment or order","s":"The court grants relief on an affidavit that a complaint with notice to defend was served and no answer was filed, or after a hearing or trial. Relief may include an order forever barring the defendant from asserting any inconsistent interest unless the defendant takes the action the order directs within thirty days, after which the prothonotary enters final judgment on the plaintiff’s praecipe; a final judgment declaring a document cancelled, valid, invalid, discharged or an authentic copy; an order directing the defendant, prothonotary or recorder of deeds to record, cancel, surrender or satisfy documents and execute what is needed; or any other proper order.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1067","t":"Quiet title; trial without jury","s":"A nonjury quiet title trial follows Rule 1038.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1068","t":"Quiet title; Acts of Assembly","s":"The rules do not suspend the 1895 Act (as amended in 1899) on recording subdivision plans, 21 P.S. §§ 399, 497–499, or Section 506 of the Mechanics’ Lien Law, 49 P.S. § 1506 (rule to file a mechanics’ lien or be barred). The 1881 Act (as amended in 1927), 21 P.S. § 688, authorizing a rule to foreclose a mortgage or be barred, is suspended absolutely.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1071","t":"Replevin; conformity to civil action","s":"Except as this subchapter provides otherwise, an action of replevin (to recover specific personal property) follows the civil action rules from commencement to judgment.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1072","t":"Replevin; venue","s":"A replevin action may be brought in any county where a civil action could be brought or in the county where the property is found.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1073","t":"Replevin; commencement of action","s":"A replevin action must be commenced by filing a complaint with the prothonotary.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1073.1","t":"Replevin; complaint; specific averments; verification","s":"The complaint must describe the property, state its value and its location if known, and set out the material facts of the claim. It must be verified by the plaintiff, or by another person with sufficient knowledge who states the source of the information and why the plaintiff did not verify.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1074","t":"Rescinded","s":"Rule 1074 (service of the replevin complaint) is rescinded; service of original process is governed by Rule 400 et seq.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1075","t":"Replevin; seizure of property before judgment","s":"The sheriff may seize the property before judgment under a writ of seizure, which may issue only on a court order entered after notice and hearing under Rule 1075.1 or ex parte under Rule 1075.2.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1075.1","t":"Writ of seizure upon notice and hearing","s":"After filing the complaint (served or not), the plaintiff may move for a writ of seizure with the complaint attached (verified if adding new facts); the hearing must be set at least 48 hours after the motion is filed, and notice in the Rule 1353 form must be served at least 24 hours before the hearing, though the court may shorten these times for perishable property or other cause. The hearing proceeds even if the defendant does not appear, and if notice was given or reasonably attempted and the plaintiff shows the probable validity of the claim, the court may order the writ upon the filing of a Rule 1075.3 bond; not appearing does not waive any defense or the right to file a counterbond. A defendant or other claimant who did not actually receive notice may petition to vacate the writ within 72 hours after seizure.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1075.2","t":"Ex parte issuance of writ of seizure","s":"After the complaint is filed, the court may issue a writ ex parte, on a bond under Rule 1075.3, if the plaintiff shows the probable validity of its claim and probable cause that before notice and hearing the property’s value will be harmed by the defendant’s continued use or the property will be concealed, disposed of, encumbered, wasted or removed from the county. The complaint and motion are served with the writ, and a hearing must be held within 72 hours after seizure; if notice was not given or reasonably attempted, or the plaintiff fails to show probable right to possession, the writ is vacated and the property returned. If the plaintiff shows a right to possession but not grounds for ex parte issuance, the plaintiff may keep possession upon paying the defendant’s reasonable expenses, which may include attorney’s fees, and a plaintiff who owes such expenses may take no further step in the action without leave of court until they are paid.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1075.3","t":"Writ of seizure; bond","s":"The writ follows the Rule 1354 form. The plaintiff’s bond must be double the value of the property averred in the complaint, with security approved by the prothonotary and the Commonwealth as obligee, conditioned on paying the value of the property and all costs, fees and damages caused by the writ if the plaintiff fails to maintain its right to possession.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1075.4","t":"Service of the writ of seizure","s":"The sheriff serves the writ (with the complaint and ex parte motion, if applicable) on the defendant and any nonparty found in possession, in the manner of Rule 402(a), and takes possession of the property. A nonparty in possession who is served becomes a defendant, the sheriff must tell that person so and note it in the return, and the plaintiff must promptly serve that person with all prior pleadings and motions under Rule 440.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1075.5","t":"Duration of validity of writ of seizure","s":"A writ of seizure remains valid until served and need not be reissued.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1076","t":"Replevin; counterbond","s":"A defendant or intervenor claiming the right to possession (but not one claiming only a lien) may file a counterbond with the prothonotary within 72 hours after seizure, or within 72 hours after service where the plaintiff waived the sheriff’s taking possession, or within any extension the court grants for cause. The counterbond must equal the original bond, with prothonotary-approved security and the Commonwealth as obligee, conditioned on paying the property’s value and all costs, fees and damages if the filer fails to maintain the right to possession; a nonparty intervening should also seek an extension because intervention usually takes more than 72 hours.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1077","t":"Replevin; disposition of seized property; sheriff’s return","s":"If the plaintiff authorizes it in writing, the sheriff may leave the property with the person in possession during the counterbond period; otherwise the sheriff holds it until that period ends, then delivers it to the plaintiff if it is not impounded, no counterbond is filed and no vacatur proceedings are pending. If a counterbond is filed, the property goes to the person in possession at seizure if that person filed one, otherwise to the first party to file one; the court may order sale or other disposition of perishable property, and the sheriff’s return must state the disposition and the name and address of anyone found in possession.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1078","t":"Replevin; exemption of property; preliminary objection","s":"A claim that property is immune or exempt from replevin must be raised by preliminary objection.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1079","t":"Replevin; impounding property","s":"Before the sheriff delivers the property to any party, any party may petition to have it impounded with the sheriff or another custodian. The court must order impoundment if money would not adequately compensate the petitioner for its loss and the petitioner posts security for storage and related expenses, which are taxed as costs; at the end of the case the property goes to the winning party.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1079.1","t":"Replevin; special equitable relief","s":"A pending replevin action does not impair any party’s right to separate and independent equitable relief.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1080","t":"Replevin; objections to bond","s":"On any party’s petition, after notice and hearing, the court may review the prothonotary’s approval or rejection of security, raise or lower any bond or require more security for cause, strike an improperly filed bond, or permit substitution of a bond and exonerate the prior one.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1081","t":"Replevin; concealment of property; examination of defendant","s":"On the plaintiff’s petition showing that the plaintiff cannot locate the property despite reasonable diligence, the sheriff cannot find it, or the defendant has concealed, removed or transferred it, the court may at any time order the defendant to be examined orally under oath about its whereabouts and enforce the order by attachment. If a writ of seizure has issued, the court may order the defendant to deliver the property to the sheriff if it is in the county or was removed to prevent recovery.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1082","t":"Replevin; counterclaim; lien; conditional verdict","s":"The only counterclaim allowed in replevin is a claim secured by a lien on the property. If any party has a lien, the court may enter a conditional verdict to protect everyone’s rights.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1083","t":"Replevin; judgment in rem when defendant is not served and does not appear","s":"If the sheriff has seized the property, the court on the plaintiff’s motion after forty-five days from seizure may enter judgment in rem for the property against any defendant who has neither been served nor appeared. A defendant who was served or appeared is instead subject to default judgment for failure to answer.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1084","t":"Replevin; judgment before trial when defendant is served or appears","s":"A pretrial judgment for the party in possession determines its right to keep the property and any special damages; one for a party not in possession determines its right to recover the property, the property’s money value based on the value stated in the complaint, and any special damages. Special damages are assessed at a trial limited to their amount.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1085","t":"Replevin; judgment after trial","s":"A judgment after trial for the party in possession determines its right to keep the property and any special damages; one for a party not in possession determines its right to recover possession, the money value of the property, and any special damages.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1086","t":"Replevin; judgment; enforcement","s":"A replevin judgment is enforced under Rules 3170 through 3173.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1087","t":"Replevin; trial without jury","s":"A nonjury replevin trial follows Rule 1038.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1088","t":"Replevin; Acts of Assembly not suspended","s":"The rules do not suspend Section 1 of the Act of May 7, 1925, 6 P.S. § 11 (determining a common law lien in replevin), or Section 7 of the Act of June 10, 1931, 3 P.S. § 635 (stray cattle).","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1091","t":"Mandamus; conformity to civil action","s":"Except as this subchapter provides otherwise, an action of mandamus (to compel performance of a public act or duty) follows the civil action rules.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1092","t":"Mandamus; venue","s":"A mandamus action by the Commonwealth on relation of the Attorney General may be brought only where the defendant political subdivision is located or where a defendant corporation has its registered office or principal place of business; one on relation of the District Attorney against a political subdivision only in its county. An action by a party with a beneficial interest distinct from the public’s may be brought only where the cause arose (against a Commonwealth officer or agency), where the political subdivision is located, or where the corporation has its registered office or principal place of business.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1093","t":"Mandamus; commencement of action","s":"A mandamus action may be commenced by filing a complaint with the prothonotary.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1094","t":"Mandamus; parties defendant","s":"To compel a political subdivision, it suffices to name the officers concerned in their official capacities; a corporation must be joined with the persons or body responsible for the duty. A Commonwealth department, board or commission, or a board of a political subdivision, may be named by itself without joining its head or members.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1095","t":"Mandamus; the complaint","s":"The complaint must state the names and descriptions of the parties, the facts relied on, the act or duty the defendant must perform and its refusal, the plaintiff’s interest in the result, any damages, the lack of any other adequate remedy at law, and a prayer for judgment commanding performance plus any damages and costs.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1096","t":"Mandamus; counterclaim","s":"No counterclaim may be asserted in mandamus.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1097","t":"Rescinded","s":"Rule 1097 (service in mandamus) is rescinded; see Rules 422 and 424 for service on the Commonwealth, political subdivisions and corporations.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1098","t":"Mandamus; peremptory judgment","s":"At any time after the complaint is filed, the court may enter judgment if the plaintiff’s right to it is clear, with prior notice to all parties unless the emergency requires acting first, in which case notice follows as soon as possible. The note says a peremptory mandamus judgment is appealable as of right without first petitioning to open it.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1099","t":"Mandamus; trial without jury","s":"A nonjury mandamus trial follows Rule 1038.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1100","t":"Mandamus; suspension of Acts of Assembly","s":"Section 1 of the Act of May 9, 1889, 52 P.S. § 1, is suspended insofar as it concerns mandamus actions to recover the bodies of entombed coal miners.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1111","t":"Quo warranto; conformity to civil action","s":"Except as this subchapter provides otherwise, an action of quo warranto (challenging the right to hold an office or exercise a franchise) follows the civil action rules.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1112","t":"Quo warranto; venue","s":"A quo warranto action on relation of the Attorney General may be brought only where the political subdivision is located (against its officer), where a municipal corporation is located (challenging its charter), or where a corporation has its registered office or principal place of business (challenging its charter, a franchise or privilege, or an officer); on relation of the District Attorney, only in the political subdivision’s county. An action by a party with an interest distinct from the public’s may be brought only where the cause arose (against a Commonwealth officer), where the political subdivision is located, or where the corporation has its registered office or principal place of business.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1113","t":"Quo warranto; commencement of action","s":"A quo warranto action must be commenced by filing a complaint with the prothonotary.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1114","t":"Quo warranto; trial without jury","s":"A nonjury quo warranto trial follows Rule 1038.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1121-1137","t":"Rescinded","s":"Rules 1121 through 1137 (Subchapter H, action of divorce) were all rescinded effective July 1, 1980.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1141","t":"Mortgage foreclosure; definition; conformity to civil action","s":"“Action” here means an action to foreclose a mortgage on an estate, leasehold or interest in land, or on both personal property and real property where the secured party elects under Section 9604(a) of the Uniform Commercial Code, but not an action to enforce personal liability. Otherwise the civil action rules apply.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1142","t":"Mortgage foreclosure; venue","s":"A foreclosure action may be brought only in a county where the land, or part of it, is located.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1143","t":"Mortgage foreclosure; commencement of action","s":"A foreclosure action must be commenced by filing a complaint with the prothonotary.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1144","t":"Mortgage foreclosure; parties; release of liability","s":"The plaintiff must name the mortgagor, the personal representative, heir or devisee of a deceased mortgagor if known, and the real owner (or, if unknown, the grantee in the last recorded deed). The mortgagor or the mortgagor’s representative, heir or devisee need not be joined, unless named as real owner, if the complaint releases that person from liability for the mortgage debt.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1145","t":"Rescinded","s":"Rule 1145 (service in foreclosure) is rescinded; service in actions involving real property is governed by Rule 410.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1146","t":"Mortgage foreclosure; pleading more than one cause of action","s":"The plaintiff may plead two or more grounds for foreclosure but only one cause of action.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1147","t":"Mortgage foreclosure; the complaint","s":"The complaint must state the parties to and date of the mortgage and any assignments and where they are recorded, describe the land, give the names, addresses and interests of the defendants (and that the real owner is unknown if not joined), specifically aver the default, give an itemized statement of the amount due, and demand judgment for it. If proceeding against both real and personal property under UCC Section 9604(a), it must also describe the personal property, which is a prerequisite to executing on both in one proceeding under Rule 3180(b); the note says residential mortgages under Act 6 of 1974 should aver compliance with Section 403 of that Act.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1148","t":"Mortgage foreclosure; counterclaim","s":"A defendant may counterclaim only on a claim arising from the same transaction or occurrence (or series) as the plaintiff’s cause of action.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1149","t":"Mortgage foreclosure; judgment; execution","s":"A foreclosure judgment is enforced under Rules 3180 through 3183.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1150","t":"Mortgage foreclosure; trial without jury","s":"A nonjury foreclosure trial follows Rule 1038.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1161","t":"Ground rent; enforcement of personal liability; conformity to civil action","s":"An in personam action to recover amounts due on a ground rent follows the civil action rules, and execution on the judgment follows the rules for enforcing money judgments.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1162","t":"Ground rent; enforcement in rem; conformity to mortgage foreclosure","s":"An in rem action to enforce a ground rent follows the mortgage foreclosure rules, substituting “ground rent,” “covenantor” and “covenantee” for “mortgage,” “mortgagor” and “mortgagee,” and the judgment is enforced like a foreclosure judgment under Rule 3180 et seq.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1163","t":"Ground rent; other remedies","s":"The procedures in Rules 1161 and 1162 are in addition to distraint for rent due, the right of entry, and any other remedy provided by law.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1164","t":"Ground rent; trial without jury","s":"A nonjury ground rent trial follows Rule 1038.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1251-1279","t":"Rescinded","s":"Rules 1251 through 1279 (Subchapter K, foreign attachment) were all rescinded effective October 1, 1989.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1285-1292","t":"Rescinded","s":"Rules 1285 through 1292 (Subchapter L, fraudulent debtor’s attachment) are all rescinded.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1301","t":"Scope (compulsory arbitration)","s":"These rules govern cases sent to compulsory arbitration under a local court rule adopted under 42 Pa.C.S. § 7361. Each court of common pleas decides by local rule whether to use arbitration, which kinds of cases go to it, and the dollar limit for arbitration, within the statutory maximum in § 7361(b).","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1302","t":"List of arbitrators; appointment to board; oath","s":"A list of available arbitrators, drawn from lawyers who practice mainly in the judicial district, is prepared as local rule directs. Each board has three lawyers appointed from the list, chaired by a lawyer admitted at least three years, with no more than one member from the same firm. An arbitrator who would be disqualified under the Code of Judicial Conduct must withdraw, and each arbitrator takes the oath required by 42 Pa.C.S. § 3151.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1303","t":"Hearing; notice","s":"Local rule sets how the hearing date, time and place are fixed, but the parties must get at least 30 days’ written notice. The notice may warn that if a party is absent, the case may be heard the same day by a judge, and there is no right to a trial de novo (a completely new trial) after a judge’s decision. If a party is not ready when the board convenes, the case proceeds to an award unless the court grants a continuance or, where that warning was given and all present parties consent, hears the case itself.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1304","t":"Conduct of hearing generally","s":"The board conducts the hearing like a non-jury trial under Rule 1038(a); the plaintiff may take a voluntary nonsuit, and the board must find for the defendant if the plaintiff fails to appear or its evidence is insufficient at the close of its case. The board may administer oaths and adjourn an unfinished hearing from day to day. No transcript or recording is made unless a party pays for one, and that party must give a copy to any other party who pays a proportionate share of the cost.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1305","t":"Conduct of hearing; evidence","s":"The rules of evidence apply, and the board rules on objections by majority vote. Certain documents (bills, business and government records, hospital and health care provider records, expert reports and qualifications, written property estimates, and employer wage-loss reports) come in without a live witness if at least 20 days’ notice and copies were given to every other party; if copies were provided at least 20 days before the hearing or in discovery, admission is up to the arbitrators absent prejudice. Any other party may subpoena the author for cross-examination at its own expense, a property estimate must state whether and by whom repairs were made, and official weather or traffic signal reports and U.S. life expectancy tables need no certification.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1306","t":"Award","s":"The board must make an award promptly after the hearing, disposing of all claims and substantially in the Rule 1312 form, with any Rule 238 delay damages stated separately. A majority must sign it (a dissent may be noted), and it is filed immediately with the prothonotary (the clerk of the civil court).","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1307","t":"Award; docketing; notice; judgment; molding the award","s":"The prothonotary dockets the award, immediately mails a copy to each party or lawyer with the date and time of entry and the arbitrators’ compensation payable on appeal, and notes the mailing date on the docket. If no appeal is filed within 30 days after entry, the prothonotary enters judgment on the award upon a praecipe (a party’s written request to the clerk). Within the 30-day appeal period a party may ask the court to mold the award to fix an obvious mathematical or language error; this stays the appeal period, and an appeal may then be filed within the 30-day period or 10 days after the court rules, whichever is later. Subdivision (b) is rescinded.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1308","t":"Appeal; arbitrators’ compensation; notice","s":"To appeal for a trial de novo, a party must file a Rule 1313 notice of appeal no later than 30 days after the day the prothonotary notes on the docket that notice of the award was sent, and must pay the arbitrators’ compensation, capped at 50% of the amount in controversy, which is not taxable as costs or recoverable. The court may, on petition, allow an appellant to waive fees and costs (wording effective November 1, 2026). The appellant supplies stamped, addressed envelopes so the prothonotary can notify the other parties (failure to give notice does not invalidate the appeal), and no bond, security, or accrued record costs are required.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1309","t":"Parties to appeal","s":"An appeal by any party is treated as an appeal by all parties on all issues unless all parties stipulate otherwise in writing.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1310","t":"Discontinuance","s":"An appeal may be discontinued only with leave of court after notice to all parties or on filing the written consent of all parties.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1311","t":"Procedure on appeal","s":"The appeal is tried de novo, and Rule 1305’s evidence shortcuts do not apply except as Rule 1311.1 allows. An arbitrator may not be called to testify about what happened before the board.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1311.1","t":"Procedure on appeal; admission of documentary evidence","s":"A plaintiff (including a counterclaiming defendant) may elect to cap its recoverable damages on appeal at the district’s compulsory-arbitration limit by filing and serving the election at least 30 days from the date the appeal is first listed for trial; the election may be withdrawn by agreement or, on the plaintiff’s motion, for good cause. If the election is made, any party may offer the Rule 1305(b)(1) documents at trial with written notice and copies given at least 20 days from the date the appeal is first listed for trial. The documents are limited to uses the absent witness’s live testimony would allow, and another party may subpoena the witness (paying usual and customary fees) or require testimony by deposition under Rule 4020(a)(5); the rule supplies forms for the election and notice.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1312","t":"Form of oath, award and notice of entry of award","s":"Sets out the form of the arbitrators’ oath, the award (with delay damages stated separately and room to note a dissent), and the prothonotary’s notice of entry of the award stating the arbitrators’ compensation payable on appeal.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1313","t":"Form of notice of appeal","s":"Sets out the notice-of-appeal form, which includes a box to demand a jury trial (otherwise waived) and a certification that the arbitrators’ compensation was paid or that permission to waive fees and costs has been sought (form amended effective November 1, 2026). No affidavit or verification is required.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1314","t":"Suspension of Acts of Assembly; abolition of practice under repealed statutes","s":"Statutes inconsistent with these rules are suspended to that extent, and compulsory-arbitration procedures from statutes repealed by the Judiciary Act Repealer Act of April 28, 1978 (JARA) do not survive as common law.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1326","t":"Definitions; scope (consumer credit arbitration)","s":"“Arbitration” means statutory arbitration under the Uniform Arbitration Act (42 Pa.C.S. § 7301 et seq.) or common law arbitration (42 Pa.C.S. § 7341 et seq.), and a “consumer credit transaction” is credit extended to an individual primarily for personal, family or household purposes. Rules 1326-1331 govern proceedings to compel arbitration and confirm awards on claims arising from consumer credit transactions.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1327","t":"Confirming arbitration award","s":"A party may move to confirm a consumer-credit arbitration award only if the party against whom it is sought attended the arbitration hearing or, after the claim was filed with the arbitrator, signed a writing agreeing to submit it, or if the award followed a court order or docket entry staying the case pending arbitration under Rule 1329.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1328","t":"Motion to confirm award as an original proceeding","s":"When the award qualifies under Rule 1327(1), the motion is filed as a new proceeding in the county where the defendant lives or does business (or else where the hearing was held), begins with the Rule 1331 notice, is served like original process, and must allege facts showing Rule 1327(1) is met. An opposing party must answer within 30 days after service; if no answer is filed, the prothonotary enters judgment on the award on praecipe, and if one is filed the court decides the motion under its motion procedures.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1329","t":"Civil action to compel arbitration; motion to confirm as ancillary to the action","s":"A plaintiff seeking to compel arbitration files a civil action whose complaint alleges an agreement to arbitrate; a default judgment is available if the defendant does not respond. If the answer admits arbitrability, either party may within 20 days file a praecipe for a docketed stay pending arbitration; if the defendant objects or denies, the plaintiff may within 20 days move for a rule to show cause (with the Rule 1330 notice), which stays the case, and the defendant must answer within 20 days raising all objections or a stay is entered on praecipe. After arbitration, a motion to confirm is filed in the same action with the Rule 1331 notice; opponents have 30 days to answer, failing which judgment is entered on praecipe.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1330-1331","t":"Forms: notices to answer","s":"Rule 1330 gives the notice for a motion to compel arbitration, warning the defendant to answer within 20 days or the case will go to arbitration. Rule 1331 gives the notice for a motion to confirm an award, warning of a 30-day answer period and possible money judgment and listing an office for help finding a lawyer; courts may require translations by local rule.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1501-1505","t":"Rescinded","s":"Rules 1501, 1502, 1503, 1504 and 1505 are rescinded. The separate action in equity has been abolished; equitable relief is sought in an ordinary civil action under Rule 1001 et seq., with Rules 1506 and 1531-1535 as special rules.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1506","t":"Stockholder’s derivative action","s":"A complaint by stockholders or members suing to enforce a right the corporation or entity has failed to enforce must allege the plaintiff’s ownership, the efforts made to get the entity to act (or why none were made), and either ownership at the time of the challenged transaction (or by operation of law from such an owner) or a strong prima facie case plus serious injustice without suit. A plaintiff relying on the second option must promptly move to maintain the action; an inadequate plaintiff is replaced or the action dismissed. The action may not be dismissed or compromised without court approval and notice to shareholders or members as the court directs; 15 Pa.C.S. § 1782(a)-(b) is suspended only where inconsistent, and § 1782(c)-(d) (including security for costs) remains in force.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1507-1530","t":"Rescinded","s":"Rules 1507 through 1530 are all rescinded. Their subjects are now covered by the general civil-action rules (for example Rules 1020, 1028, 1031, 1037, 1038, 227.1 and 3021).","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1531","t":"Special relief; injunctions","s":"A preliminary or special injunction requires written notice and a hearing unless immediate and irreparable injury would occur first, and the court may rely on pleadings, affidavits or other proof. Except when the plaintiff is the Commonwealth, a political subdivision, or one of their agencies or officers, the plaintiff must file a court-approved bond naming the Commonwealth as obligee (or deposit cash with the prothonotary) to cover damages, costs and fees if the injunction is dissolved as improper. Any party may move to dissolve at any time; an injunction granted without notice is dissolved unless a continuance hearing is held within 5 days (or as agreed or ordered), and for injunctions affecting freedom of expression the court must hold a final hearing within 3 days after the defendant demands one, file a final order within 24 hours after the hearing (or the injunction dissolves), and file a supporting memorandum within 5 days.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1532","t":"Special relief; perpetuation of testimony","s":"A complaint to preserve testimony for a contemplated action must name the prospective parties, describe the action and the need to preserve the testimony, and identify each witness and the expected substance. Testimony may be taken before the court or by deposition, and the final order says whether it is preserved. Preserved testimony may be used at trial like a Rule 4020 deposition, including by or against successors in interest, and in any Pennsylvania county.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1533","t":"Special relief; receivers","s":"A temporary receiver may be appointed without notice if the situation requires, but (unless a statute provides otherwise) only after the plaintiff posts a court-approved bond or cash deposit; a prompt hearing on continuing the appointment must follow, with notice by the receiver to interested persons including known creditors and stockholders. A corporate officer or partner cannot be sole temporary receiver; every receiver must post security before acting; an order to operate a business (other than a public utility) must be for a fixed, extendable period. On appointing a permanent receiver the court also appoints two appraisers to inventory the assets and fixes a deadline for the receiver’s report; statutory receivers such as the Secretary of Banking and Insurance Commissioner keep their statutory duties.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1534","t":"Accounting by fiduciaries","s":"Unless a statute provides otherwise, a receiver, assignee or other fiduciary filing an account must give notice of the date set for confirmation, stating that the account may be confirmed and distribution ordered unless exceptions are filed first. Notice is given by personal service, mail or publication as local rule or order directs, and the court may order partial distributions and require refunding bonds.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1535","t":"Objections to security","s":"On petition, notice and hearing, the court may for cause increase or decrease required security, strike an improperly filed bond, or allow substitute security and release the original.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1536","t":"Rescinded","s":"Rule 1536 is rescinded.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1549","t":"Acts of Assembly not suspended","s":"Lists statutes that the civil-action rules do not suspend, including laws on timber cutting by co-owners, insolvency, insurance and bank receiverships, Labor Relations Board enforcement, labor-dispute injunctions, Clean Streams Law enforcement, corporate-dissolution receivers (15 Pa.C.S. §§ 1984-1986), and Public Utility Code injunctions (66 Pa.C.S. § 502).","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1550","t":"Acts of Assembly suspended","s":"Suspends the venue provisions of three statutes on injunctions: the seed-labeling law (3 P.S. § 297) and two mining laws (52 P.S. § 701-121 and 52 P.S. § 70-1201).","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1551","t":"Partition: form of action","s":"Except as this subchapter provides, an action to partition (divide) real estate follows the ordinary civil-action rules.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1552","t":"Partition: venue","s":"A partition action, including one involving the Commonwealth, may be brought only in a county where all or part of the property is located.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1553","t":"Partition: parties","s":"Any one or more co-tenants (co-owners) may sue for partition, and all other co-tenants must be joined as defendants.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1554","t":"Partition: specific averments","s":"The complaint must describe the property and state the nature and extent of each party’s interest.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1555","t":"Partition: pleading more than one cause of action","s":"The plaintiff may seek partition of any properties co-owned with the defendants anywhere in Pennsylvania, whatever the plaintiff’s share in each. If all parties’ rights come from a common source of title, claims may be joined even if some defendants co-own fewer than all the properties.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1556","t":"Partition: counterclaim","s":"A defendant may counterclaim for partition of any property the plaintiff could have included in the complaint.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1557","t":"Order directing partition; post-trial relief","s":"If the court decides partition is warranted (by default, admission, hearing or trial), it enters an order directing partition that names all co-tenants and their interests. No exceptions may be filed to that order; under the official note it is appealable as of right, though post-trial motions are still required for orders that do not direct partition or that resolve other claims.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1558","t":"Preliminary conference; appointment of hearing officer","s":"After the order directing partition, the court holds a preliminary conference on whether the parties can agree on a plan of partition or sale, simplifying issues, referring matters to a hearing officer, and other matters. Afterward the court may appoint a hearing officer to hear the whole matter, conduct a sale, or handle specified issues.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1559","t":"Hearing officer; hearing","s":"A hearing officer conducts the needed examinations and hearings on reasonable notice and may hire appraisers and, with court approval, other experts.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1560","t":"Property capable of division without prejudice","s":"If the property can be divided without spoiling the whole, it is divided first into as many purparts (shares of land) as there are parties, proportionate in value to their interests; if that is impossible, into that many purparts regardless of value; and if that too is impossible, into whatever number is most advantageous.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1561","t":"Property capable of proportionate division; award","s":"Purparts proportionate in value under Rule 1560(a) are awarded to the parties according to their interests.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1562","t":"Property not capable of proportionate division; award","s":"Property divisible only under Rule 1560(b) or (c) is awarded equitably among the parties with owelty (cash payments to equalize unequal shares), subject to acceptance, rejection and private sale among the parties; the court sets how owelty is secured and paid.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1563","t":"Property not capable of division without prejudice; sale; objections","s":"Property that cannot be divided without spoiling the whole is offered for private sale limited to the parties. However, defendants owning a majority in value may object in writing and have the whole property awarded to them as tenants in common at the court’s valuation, with the amounts owed to the parties seeking partition becoming liens payable as the court directs.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1564","t":"Life estates","s":"Property subject to a life estate may be partitioned if security is posted to ensure the life tenant receives the annual rental value, interest or income. If a life tenant has exclusive possession of part of the property, the court may refuse partition of that part or partition it subject to that possession.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1565","t":"Retention of undivided interests; election; parties not appearing","s":"The court must let two or more co-tenants keep their shares together if they so elect in a writing filed within the time set by the court or hearing officer. The court may also keep together the shares of co-tenants who do not appear.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1566","t":"Preliminary determination; notice to accept or reject; private sale confined to parties","s":"Parties receive preliminary notice, as local rule or order directs, of a proposed division under Rule 1560(b) or (c) or of a finding that the property cannot be divided. For a proposed division, parties have 20 days after service to accept or reject; for indivisible property, the notice warns of sale unless majority owners object under Rule 1563(b). The notice must describe the property, plan, valuations and encumbrances (or say where they may be examined in the county), and if anyone rejects the plan or no one objects to sale, the property is offered by open bidding among the parties on at least 20 days’ notice.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1567","t":"Private sale confined to parties; conduct; confirmation","s":"Divided property is offered both by purparts and as a whole to see which brings more. No sale is confirmed unless the bid at least equals the court’s valuation of the whole or of the purpart, and awards are subject to owelty where required.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1568","t":"Public sale","s":"If a private sale among the parties is not confirmed, the property or purpart is sold at public sale or private sale open to outsiders, as the court directs.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1569","t":"Hearing officer’s report; exceptions","s":"The hearing officer files a report following the Rule 1570 decision format, after giving all interested persons written notice of the intended filing date and where the report and proposed order may be examined (with written notice of any later changes). Any party may file exceptions within 10 days after notice of filing, and the court may remand or enter a decision adopting the report in whole or part.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1570","t":"Decision and order","s":"The decision must find, among other things, whether and how the property can be divided, the values of the whole and the purparts, encumbrances, credits and charges for use, taxes, rents and services, whether interests remain undivided, the parties’ acceptance or bids, and whether an outside sale is needed and which kind will bring a better price. The order must award the property or purparts (with owelty amounts, timing and security), protect life tenants, unborn or unknown remaindermen and missing persons, and order any needed sale.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1571","t":"Trustees to satisfy liens and charges","s":"On motion or the hearing officer’s recommendation, the court may appoint a trustee to receive sums owed to unknown or missing lienholders, principal needed to secure charges on the property, or sale proceeds of property subject to a life estate and remainder. After posting security and receiving payment, the trustee may satisfy the lien of record, freeing the property.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1572","t":"Sale not confined to parties","s":"An outside sale is conducted as local rule or the sale order directs, subject to resale for inadequate price, and a public sale must be advertised in each county where any part of the property lies. A purchasing party or discharged lienholder gets credit for its distributive share, paying the excess in cash, and a hearing officer conducting the sale must post bond in double the payment amount, or a lower amount set by the court, before accepting payment.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1573","t":"Return of sale and schedule of distribution","s":"A hearing officer who conducts a sale promptly files a return of sale and proposed order confirming the sale, authorizing deeds, protecting life tenants and other absent interests, directing distribution, and providing for costs, after written notice to all interested persons. If the court approves, any part of its order not challenged by a post-trial motion within 10 days becomes final.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1574","t":"Costs and counsel fees","s":"Costs, including appraisers’, hearing officer’s and court-authorized experts’ fees, are paid by the parties in proportion to their interests. Reasonable counsel fees may be charged against the property or fund and apportioned as the court finds equitable.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1576-1577","t":"Rescinded","s":"Rules 1576 and 1577 (actions to prevent waste) are rescinded; a claim to prevent waste is brought as a civil action seeking equitable relief.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1580-1581","t":"Rescinded","s":"Rules 1580 and 1581 are rescinded; Rule 1580’s content moved to Rule 1549(1), and Rule 1581 suspended statutes that were later repealed.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1590","t":"Partition; Acts of Assembly not suspended","s":"The partition rules do not affect the 1895 statute (68 P.S. § 101) requiring co-tenants in possession to account for rental value owed to co-tenants out of possession, or the 1943 statute (16 P.S. §§ 9905-9906) on registering partition titles with county Registry Survey Bureaus.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1591","t":"Rescinded","s":"Rule 1591 is rescinded.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1601","t":"Action for declaratory relief alone; jury trial; waiver","s":"A plaintiff seeking only declaratory relief files a complaint captioned “Action for Declaratory Judgment,” and the case follows the civil-action rules as nearly as possible. Any right to a jury on disputed facts is waived unless demanded in the time and manner Rule 1007.1 requires; the rule itself does not decide whether such a right exists.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1602","t":"Declaratory judgment as ancillary relief","s":"In any civil action, a party may include a request for declaratory relief, and the procedure follows the rules for that action.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1603","t":"Effective date; pending action","s":"These rules applied to actions pending when they took effect; the court could let cases proceed on pleadings filed under the old practice or allow or require amended or supplemental pleadings.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1604","t":"Suspension of Acts of Assembly; abolition of practice under repealed statutes","s":"Inconsistent statutes are suspended to that extent, and declaratory-judgment procedures from statutes repealed by JARA (repeals effective June 27, 1978, June 27, 1979 and June 27, 1980) do not continue as common law.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1701","t":"Definitions; conformity","s":"A “class action” is any action by or against parties as representatives of a class until the court refuses or revokes certification, and “residual funds” are what remains after paying approved class claims, expenses, costs, attorney’s fees and other approved disbursements. Otherwise, a class action follows the rules for the underlying form of action.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1702","t":"Prerequisites to a class action","s":"Class members may sue or be sued as representatives only if the class is so numerous that joinder is impracticable, there are common questions of law or fact, the representatives’ claims or defenses are typical, the representatives will fairly and adequately protect the class under Rule 1709, and a class action is a fair and efficient method of adjudication under Rule 1708.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1703","t":"Commencement of action; assignment to a judge","s":"A class action may be started only by filing a complaint with the prothonotary, not by writ of summons. On filing, it is immediately assigned to one judge for all purposes.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1704","t":"Form of the complaint","s":"The caption must say “Class Action,” and a separate section titled “Class Action Allegations” must plead facts supporting Rule 1702 and the Rule 1708 and 1709 criteria. The complaint may join equitable, declaratory and money claims arising from the same transaction or series of transactions.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1705","t":"Preliminary objections","s":"All Rule 1028(a) preliminary objections must be raised at once. Factual disputes about the Class Action Allegations cannot be raised by preliminary objection and must be raised in the answer.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1706","t":"Form of the answer","s":"Factual averments under “Class Action Allegations” are deemed admitted unless denied as Rule 1029 requires.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1706.1","t":"Joinder of additional defendants; cross-claims","s":"A defendant may join a new additional defendant, or cross-claim against another party, only if that person may be solely liable, liable over to the joining party, or jointly or severally liable on the plaintiff’s cause of action. This is narrower than general joinder under Rule 2252(a) and cross-claims under Rule 1031.1.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1707","t":"Motion for certification; time for filing; hearing","s":"The plaintiff must move for class certification within 30 days after the pleadings close or within 30 days after the last required pleading was due; the court may extend the time for cause, and if no motion is filed the court, once notified, promptly sets a certification hearing. The hearing may be postponed for other motions or class discovery, is limited to the Class Action Allegations, and the court considers all relevant testimony, depositions, admissions and other evidence.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1708","t":"Criteria: class action as fair and efficient method","s":"For money claims, the court weighs whether common questions predominate, class size and manageability, risks of inconsistent or practically dispositive separate rulings, other pending litigation, the suitability of the forum, whether individual claims are too small to sue on separately, and whether recoveries would be too small to justify the expense of a class action. For equitable or declaratory relief alone, it weighs the first five of these factors and whether the opposing party acted on grounds generally applicable to the class; where both kinds of relief are sought, it weighs all of them.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1709","t":"Criteria: fair and adequate representation","s":"The court considers whether class counsel will adequately represent the class, whether the representatives have a conflict of interest, and whether they have or can get enough financial resources to protect the class.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1710","t":"Order certifying or refusing to certify; revocation; amendment; findings","s":"An order certifying, refusing or revoking certification must be accompanied by an opinion with findings of fact and conclusions of law on Rules 1702, 1708 and 1709, and a certification order must describe the class. The court may limit the class action to particular issues or forms of relief or create subclasses, and may make the order conditional and change it before a merits decision, with a memorandum of reasons. If certification is refused or revoked, the case continues by or against the named parties only.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1711","t":"The plaintiff class; exclusion; inclusion","s":"Ordinarily every member of a certified plaintiff class is included unless the member files a written election to opt out by a date the court sets. The court may instead require members to opt in by a specified date if individual claims are substantial and members have the resources and sophistication to sue on their own, or if other special circumstances described in the order exist.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1712","t":"Order; notice of action","s":"After certification and a hearing on notice, the court enters an order setting the type and content of notice and which members get it, weighing class size, relief sought, cost and possible prejudice. The court may require individual notice by personal service or mail to members identifiable with reasonable effort, and otherwise notice by methods such as newspaper, television, radio, or trade, union or public-interest groups. The plaintiff prepares and pays for notice; defendants may object to the proposed form within 10 days, the court may require a defendant to use its own communication channels with members at the plaintiff’s cost, and combined notice costs for a counterclaim against the class are allocated by the court.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1713","t":"Conduct of actions","s":"The court may issue orders to manage the case, require additional notice to members of any step or proposed judgment, allow intervention under Rule 2326 et seq., impose conditions on representatives or intervenors, ensure adequate representation, and handle other procedural matters; these orders may be changed from time to time.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1714","t":"Compromise; settlement; discontinuance","s":"No class action may be settled, compromised or discontinued without court approval after a hearing. Before certification, the representative may discontinue without notice to the class if the court finds no prejudice to other members; after certification, notice of the proposed settlement or discontinuance goes to all members as the court directs. A settlement need not create residual funds.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1715","t":"Judgment","s":"Except by special order, no default, judgment on the pleadings, or summary judgment may be entered for or against the class until the court rules on certification. A judgment on preliminary objections before certification binds only the named parties, while a judgment in a certified class action binds all members unless the court directs otherwise; the court frames every judgment and identifies who is bound.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1716","t":"Residual funds","s":"Any judgment or settlement order that sets up a process to identify and pay class members must provide for disbursing residual funds. At least 50% of residual funds must go to the Pennsylvania Interest on Lawyers Trust Account Board to support civil legal aid for the poor through nonprofit 501(c)(3) organizations; the rest may go to that Board or to another entity whose purposes relate to the objectives of the case or otherwise serve the class members’ interests.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"1717","t":"Counsel fees","s":"When the court is authorized by law to set counsel fees, it considers, among other things, the time and effort reasonably spent, the quality of services, the results and benefits to the class or public, the size, complexity and uniqueness of the case, and whether the fee was contingent on success. The rule does not decide when fees may be awarded, which is a matter of substantive law, and the factors are not listed in order of weight.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"4001","t":"Scope; definitions","s":"The discovery rules apply to any civil action or proceeding in a court subject to these rules, including eminent domain and Municipal Claims Act cases and cases referred to compulsory arbitration; “court” means the court where the case is pending, and “trial” includes a hearing before arbitrators or viewers. Any party may depose any person, including a party, orally or by written interrogatories for discovery, pleading preparation, trial preparation, or use at a hearing. The listed discovery methods are oral or written depositions, interrogatories to a party, document production and property entry, physical and mental examinations, and requests for admission; unlike the federal rules, there is no automatic initial-disclosure requirement.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"4002","t":"Agreement regarding discovery or deposition procedure","s":"The parties may agree that depositions be taken before any person, at any time or place, on any notice and in any manner (and then used like other depositions), and may agree to modify the procedures for any discovery method.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"4002.1","t":"Filing discovery material","s":"Discovery materials are not filed with the court unless relevant to a motion or other pretrial proceeding, ordered by the court, or required by statute.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"4003","t":"Rescinded","s":"Rule 4003 (right to take depositions; notice) is rescinded; its content moved to Rules 4001(c), 4007.1 and 4007.2.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"4003.1","t":"Scope of discovery generally; opinions and contentions","s":"Subject to Rules 4003.2-4003.5 and 4011, a party may discover any non-privileged matter relevant to the subject matter of the case, whether it relates to its own or another party’s claim or defense, including documents and the identity and location of people with knowledge. Inadmissibility at trial is no objection if the information appears reasonably calculated to lead to admissible evidence, and requests calling for opinions or contentions about facts or the application of law to fact are allowed. Pennsylvania keeps this “subject matter” and “reasonably calculated” standard in the rule text rather than the current federal claims-and-defenses proportionality wording; the official note urges sparing use of broad contention interrogatories.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"4003.2","t":"Scope of discovery; insurance","s":"A party may discover whether any insurance agreement exists that may cover all or part of a judgment, and its terms. Disclosure does not make the information admissible at trial, and an insurance application is not part of the agreement.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"4003.3","t":"Scope of discovery; trial preparation material generally","s":"Material prepared in anticipation of litigation by or for another party or its representative (lawyer, consultant, insurer, agent and the like) is discoverable if otherwise within Rule 4003.1, with no requirement to show substantial need. Protected are the attorney’s mental impressions, conclusions, opinions, memoranda, notes, summaries, legal research and legal theories, and, for non-attorney representatives, their mental impressions, conclusions or opinions about the value or merit of a claim or defense or about strategy or tactics.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"4003.4","t":"Scope of discovery; trial preparation material; statements","s":"On written request, a party is entitled to immediate receipt of a copy of any prior statement about the case made by that party, any other party, or a witness, and a non-party may obtain a copy of their own statement; if refused, they may move for a court order. A “statement” is a signed or adopted written statement, or a substantially verbatim recording or transcript made at the time.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"4003.5","t":"Discovery of expert testimony; trial preparation material","s":"Expert discovery proceeds by interrogatory: a party may require others to identify each expected trial expert and the subject matter, and to have the expert state the substance of the facts and opinions and a summary of the grounds, answered by a signed expert report or the expert’s signed answers; further discovery (such as an expert deposition) requires court order on cause shown, so there are no automatic federal-style expert reports or depositions. Non-testifying consulting experts are discoverable only under Rule 4010(b) for medical experts or on a court finding of exceptional circumstances, and communications between a party’s attorney and its experts, including draft reports, are protected except where privileged communications could be disclosed. An expert not identified as required may not testify (absent extenuating circumstances beyond the party’s control), and an expert’s trial testimony may not be inconsistent with or go beyond the fair scope of the discovery responses, though the expert may address matters never asked about.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"4003.6","t":"Discovery of treating physician","s":"Information may be obtained from a party’s treating physician only with that party’s written consent or through a discovery method authorized by these rules. This does not stop a lawyer from getting information from the lawyer’s own client or the client’s actual or ostensible employee.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"4003.7","t":"Punitive damages","s":"Discovery of a defendant’s wealth on a punitive-damages claim requires a court order setting limits on the timing, scope and dissemination of the discovery, though the parties may agree otherwise under Rule 4002.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"4003.8","t":"Pre-complaint discovery","s":"A plaintiff may take discovery before filing a complaint if the information is material and necessary to file it and the discovery will not cause unreasonable annoyance, embarrassment, oppression, burden or expense. On a protective-order motion or objection, the court may require the plaintiff to explain with particularity how the discovery will materially advance the complaint and must weigh its importance against the burden.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"4004","t":"Procedure on depositions by written interrogatories","s":"The party taking a deposition on written questions serves them on every party, with notice of the officer, time, place and deponents (an organization may be named under Rule 4007.1(e)); cross-interrogatories may be served within 30 days, and later rounds within 10 days. Objections to form are waived unless served within the time for the next round or within 10 days after the last interrogatories. The officer asks the questions, certifies the deposition and sends it by registered mail, the receiving party notifies all others, and the court may issue protective orders or require oral examination instead.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"4005","t":"Written interrogatories to a party","s":"Subject to Rule 4011, any party may serve interrogatories on any other party (an organization answers through an officer or agent with available information), starting at service of original process; interrogatories served before the complaint must be limited to preparing it and briefly state the cause of action, and space must be left after each question for the answer. Interrogatories may cover anything within Rules 4003.1-4003.5 and may be used at trial like a party’s deposition. The rule sets no fixed numerical limit, but the court may limit the number of interrogatories or sets as justice requires to prevent unreasonable burden; subdivision (b) is rescinded.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"4006","t":"Answers to written interrogatories by a party","s":"Answers must be written, verified, inserted in the spaces provided, and complete unless objected to with stated reasons; answers are signed by the person answering and objections by the attorney, and an objection does not excuse answering the rest. Answers and objections must be served within 30 days after service of the interrogatories, and the asking party may move to dismiss an objection. If the answer can be derived from the answering party’s records with equal burden either way, the party may specify the records and allow inspection and copying.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"4007","t":"Rescinded","s":"Rule 4007 is rescinded; its content moved to Rules 4001(c), 4003.1, 4007.1 and 4007.2.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"4007.1","t":"Procedure in deposition by oral examination","s":"A party must give every other party reasonable written notice (except a defendant served by publication who has not appeared) stating the time, place and deponent, and a party noticed for deposition must appear without a subpoena. In pre-complaint depositions after a writ of summons, the notice must briefly state the cause of action and topics; a party deponent may be asked to bring documents under Rule 4009.1 et seq., and a non-party subpoenaed to bring materials must produce them at the deposition, not earlier, unless all parties consent. A party may name an organization as deponent and describe the topics with reasonable particularity, and the organization must designate people to testify on matters known or reasonably available to it.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"4007.2","t":"When leave of court required","s":"Depositions generally need no court permission, except under Rule 1042.5 (professional liability before a certificate of merit), Rule 4003.5(a)(2) (further expert discovery), and this rule. A plaintiff needs leave to depose someone within 30 days after service of original process unless the defendant has already sought discovery or the deponent is aged or infirm or about to leave for a place outside Pennsylvania or more than 100 miles from the courthouse, in which case the notice must state the supporting facts, certified by the plaintiff’s attorney’s signature. Deposing a prisoner always requires leave of court.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"4007.3","t":"Sequence and timing of discovery","s":"Unless the court orders otherwise, discovery methods may be used in any order, and one party’s discovery does not delay another’s.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"4007.4","t":"Supplementing responses","s":"There is generally no duty to supplement a complete response, except that a party must seasonably supplement answers about people with knowledge and about expected trial experts, their subject matter and the substance of their testimony. A party or expert must seasonably amend a response learned to be incorrect when made or no longer true, and further duties may arise by court order, agreement, or new requests before trial.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"4008","t":"Oral examination; limitation","s":"If an oral deposition is to be taken more than 100 miles from the courthouse, the court may on motion order payment of reasonable expenses, including attorney’s fees.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"4009","t":"Rescinded","s":"Rule 4009 (production of documents and entry for inspection) is rescinded; the subject is now covered by Rules 4009.1-4009.33.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"4009.1","t":"Production of documents and things; general provisions","s":"A party may request documents, tangible things and electronically stored information (ESI) from a party under Rules 4009.11-4009.12, or subpoena them from a non-party under Rules 4009.21-4009.27, to inspect, copy, test or sample items within Rules 4003.1-4003.6 that are in the recipient’s possession, custody or control, one or more times. The requester may specify an ESI format, subject to objection; otherwise ESI may be produced as ordinarily maintained or in a reasonably usable form. The official comment states ESI discovery is governed by a proportionality standard and does not import federal ESI case law.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"4009.11","t":"Request upon a party for production","s":"A document request may be served without leave of court on the plaintiff after the action begins and on any other party with or after service of original process. It must list items in numbered paragraphs, each seeking a single item or category described with reasonable particularity, with space for the answer after each.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"4009.12","t":"Answer to request upon a party for production","s":"Within 30 days after service, the responding party must answer each paragraph (including objections) and produce the unobjected-to items, or, where finding them requires reviewing a larger group with equal burden either way, let the requester review that group. The answer must identify what is produced, identify with reasonable particularity anything withheld as outside the scope of discovery and the basis, object on Rule 4011 or form grounds, or state that no responsive documents exist after reasonable investigation. It is signed and verified by the party (and signed by counsel for objections), and if a request is ambiguous the party must either produce or identify what is withheld.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"4009.21","t":"Subpoena upon a non-party for production; prior notice; objections","s":"A party seeking documents from a non-party must give every other party written notice, with a copy of the proposed subpoena, at least 20 days before serving it (the non-party is not notified). Any party may file and serve written objections; if objections arrive before service, the subpoena may not be served until the court rules on a motion, and if none arrive, it may be served subject to any party’s or interested person’s right to seek a protective order. Unlike federal practice, which requires only advance notice to other parties, this rule builds in a 20-day objection window that blocks service until objections are resolved.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"4009.22","t":"Service of subpoena","s":"The subpoena may be served only if it is identical to the one attached to the notice and the party has filed a certificate that the notice was mailed or delivered to each party at least 20 days before service, that a copy is attached, that no objection was received, and that the subpoena is identical. The subpoena is issued by the prothonotary and served under Rule 234.2; the official note says the 20-day period may be waived.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"4009.23","t":"Certificate of compliance by a non-party; notice of documents received","s":"A subpoenaed non-party must deliver the documents or things with a signed certificate of compliance to the serving party within 20 days of service, and a blank certificate must be served with the subpoena. The receiving party must notify all other parties and, on payment of reasonable cost, provide legible copies or reasonable access to things on request.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"4009.24-4009.27","t":"Forms for non-party document subpoenas","s":"These rules give the forms for the notice of intent to serve a subpoena (stating the 20-day objection period), objections to a subpoena, the certificate prerequisite to service, the subpoena itself (ordering production within 20 days after service and noting the right to seek reasonable copying costs in advance), and the non-party’s certificate of compliance.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"4009.31","t":"Entry upon property for inspection; general provisions","s":"A party may request entry onto a party’s property under Rule 4009.32, or move for entry onto a non-party’s property under Rule 4009.33, to inspect, measure, survey, photograph, test or sample the property or objects or operations on it, within Rules 4003.1-4003.6.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"4009.32","t":"Request for entry upon property of a party","s":"The request may be served without leave on the plaintiff after the case begins and on other parties with or after original process, and must describe the property and activities with reasonable particularity. The responding party must allow entry unless it objects with reasons within 30 days after service; the requester may seek a Rule 4019(a) order on objections or noncompliance, and entry may occur more than once to complete the requested activities.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"4009.33","t":"Motion for entry upon property of a non-party","s":"Entry onto a non-party’s land requires a motion beginning with the prescribed notice, describing the property and activities, personally served by an adult like original process and served on all parties. If the non-party does not affirmatively consent, the motion may be presented to the court on at least 15 days’ notice to the non-party, and the court may grant or deny entry or set a hearing, specifying reasonable time, manner and conditions; the rule supplies the notice form.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"4010","t":"Physical and mental examination of persons","s":"When a party’s (or a person in a party’s custody or legal control’s) mental or physical condition is in controversy, the court may, on motion for good cause and notice, order an examination by a licensed physician, dentist or psychologist, specifying time, place, manner, conditions, scope and examiner. The person examined may have counsel or a representative present, questioning is limited to the scope of the exam, and the examined party may record it at its own expense (these two protections do not apply in custody or visitation cases). On request, the party causing the exam must provide a detailed report (and earlier reports on the same condition), then becomes entitled to the other side’s like reports; requesting the report or deposing the examiner waives privilege as to others who examined the same condition, and an examiner who will not report is barred from testifying.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"4010.1","t":"Evaluation of earning capacity","s":"When a party’s earning capacity is in controversy, the court may order an evaluation by a suitably licensed or certified evaluator, subject to the procedures of Rule 4010(a)(3)-(b)(3). The evaluator may testify only on damages, not liability.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"4011","t":"Limitation of scope of discovery","s":"No discovery, including ESI discovery, is allowed if it is sought in bad faith, would cause unreasonable annoyance, embarrassment, oppression, burden or expense to anyone, is beyond the scope of Rules 4003.1-4003.6, is barred by law protecting mediation communications and documents (42 Pa.C.S. § 5949), or would require an unreasonable investigation by the deponent or any party or witness.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"4012","t":"Protective orders","s":"On motion and good cause, the court may protect a party or person from unreasonable annoyance, embarrassment, oppression, burden or expense, for example by barring discovery, setting terms, time and place, requiring a different method, excluding topics, limiting scope or attendance, sealing depositions, or protecting trade secrets and confidential commercial information; if the motion is denied, the court may order discovery. During a deposition, the court may stop or limit it, and on demand the deposition is suspended long enough to seek a ruling.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"4013","t":"Stay of proceedings","s":"Filing a protective-order motion does not stay the challenged discovery unless the court orders, but the court may for good cause stay any or all proceedings until the motion is decided.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"4014","t":"Request for admission","s":"A party may serve written requests to admit the truth of matters within Rules 4003.1-4003.5, including facts, opinions of fact, application of law to fact, and the genuineness of documents (with copies served unless already available), on the plaintiff after the action begins and on others with or after original process. Each matter is admitted unless a verified answer or signed objection is served within 30 days after service (or as the court allows), but a defendant need not respond until 45 days after service of original process; answers must admit, deny, or explain specifically, lack of knowledge requires a statement of reasonable inquiry, and a genuine trial issue alone is no ground for objection. The requester may move to test sufficiency, and admissions are conclusive for that case only unless the court permits withdrawal or amendment when it serves the merits without prejudice.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"4015","t":"Persons before whom depositions may be taken","s":"In the United States and its territories, depositions are taken before an officer authorized to administer oaths under federal, Pennsylvania or local law, or a court-appointed person. Abroad, they may be taken on notice before a person authorized locally, before a court-commissioned person, or by letter rogatory, and letter-rogatory evidence is not excluded merely for being non-verbatim or unsworn. No deposition may be taken before a relative, employee or attorney of a party, a relative or employee of such attorney, or anyone financially interested in the action.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"4016","t":"Taking of depositions; objections","s":"Objections to the officer’s qualification are waived unless made at the start or as soon as the problem is or should have been discovered. Objections to competency, relevancy or materiality are preserved for trial unless the ground could have been cured if raised at the deposition, while errors in manner, form, oath or conduct that could have been cured are waived without timely objection, and notice defects are waived unless written objection is promptly served.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"4017","t":"Transcript of testimony; objections","s":"The officer swears the witness and records the testimony, which is transcribed; objections to the transcript are waived unless filed promptly once known. The witness reads and signs (noting changes and reasons) unless waived or the witness is ill, missing or refuses; if not signed within 30 days of submission, the officer signs, states why, and the deposition may be used as if signed unless the court rules otherwise. The officer certifies the transcript, parties may submit written questions in lieu of attending, and copies are available to any party or the deponent for reasonable charges.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"4017.1","t":"Video depositions","s":"Any oral deposition may be videotaped as of course, with optional simultaneous stenographic recording, but it may be used in court only with a transcript. The notice or subpoena must state it is a video deposition and list the deponent, officer, whether there will be a stenographic record, and the operator and employer (who may be the deposing attorney’s employee); the operator makes on-camera announcements, a digital clock shows hours, minutes and seconds on screen, and no witness signature is needed. The deposing attorney keeps custody and provides copies at a requesting party’s cost; a video deposition of a medical witness or a non-party expert may be used at trial for any purpose even if the witness is available.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"4018","t":"Rescinded","s":"Rule 4018 is rescinded; see Rule 234.1 et seq. for subpoenas.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"4019","t":"Sanctions","s":"On motion, the court may act when a party fails to answer interrogatories, an organization fails to designate a deponent, a deponent fails to appear or a witness is induced not to, a court order is disobeyed, document inspection is refused, or discovery is otherwise not made; failure is not excused because the discovery was objectionable unless an objection or protective-order motion was filed. Sanctions include deeming facts established, precluding claims, defenses or evidence, striking pleadings, staying proceedings, entering non pros or default, contempt (but not for refusing a physical or mental exam), or other just orders; a deponent who refuses to answer may be compelled by court order. The court may also shift expenses and attorney’s fees for unjustified failures to admit, no-shows at noticed depositions, failure to subpoena a witness, disobeyed compliance orders, unjustified sanctions motions, and motions filed for delay or in bad faith (which bar further steps until paid); an undisclosed witness may not testify absent extenuating circumstances, and fees may not be imposed on the Commonwealth.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"4020","t":"Use of depositions at trial","s":"At trial, an admissible deposition may be used against any party present, represented or properly noticed: by any party to contradict or impeach, or as the Rules of Evidence allow; by an adverse party for any purpose if the deponent was a party or its officer, director, managing agent or designee; and by any party for any purpose if the witness is dead, more than 100 miles away or outside Pennsylvania (unless procured by the offering party), unable to attend due to age, sickness, infirmity or imprisonment, unobtainable by subpoena, or exceptional circumstances justify it. An oral deposition of a non-party medical witness may be used for any purpose even if the witness is available, other parties may require the rest of a relevant deposition to be introduced, and depositions survive substitution of parties and may be used in a later action on the same subject between the same parties or successors. Trial objections may be made as if the witness were present (subject to Rule 4016(b)), and taking a deposition does not make the deponent the taker’s own witness.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"4023","t":"Acts of Assembly not suspended","s":"The rules do not affect 42 Pa.C.S. § 5325 on depositions outside Pennsylvania, or statutes on shareholder inspection of corporate records (15 Pa.C.S. § 1508), examinations in insolvency proceedings, election contests, and appeals from registration commissions.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"4024","t":"Effective date; pending actions","s":"The November 20, 1978 amendments, effective April 15, 1979, applied to all actions pending on April 15, 1979.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"},{"n":"4025","t":"Abolition of practice under repealed statutes","s":"Deposition and discovery procedures from statutes repealed by the Judiciary Act Repealer Act (Act of April 28, 1978, No. 53) are abolished and do not continue as common law.","u":"https://www.pacodeandbulletin.gov/Display/pacode?file=/secure/pacode/data/231/231toc.html"}]});
